{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/autonomous_agent_architect.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"alternative_architecture\": {\n    \"description\": \"Reorganize around one feature-delivery transaction rather than a hierarchy of global planning and evidence systems. State: keep product canon/design decisions separate from pipeline policy and repository inventory; use a small approved feature contract, Git commit/tree, and one append-only attempt journal as the principal records. Planning: a human selects priority, then one bounded planning/impact pass identifies relevant requirements, files, tests, and unresolved decisions just in time. Execution: a restartable state machine moves one worktree through selected → implementing → validating → human review → merged/blocked, using one write-capable coding worker initially. Validation: task-specific Git scope checks, Unity compilation/tests, clean-tree verification, fresh semantic review when useful, and explicit human Play Mode criteria produce one gate report attached to the candidate commit. Recovery: preserve the worktree, commit, failed gate results, retry count, and next action; measure progress by resolved gates and relevant diffs, not merely any changed file. GDD change: create a reviewed change event mapping stable requirement IDs to affected contracts, tests, and implementation surfaces; replan or revalidate only those affected. Unity: use an actual Editor/host runner, in-memory or temporary test assets, committed-scene conformance checks, meta preservation, and mandatory human visual/feel review. Reconciliation becomes an occasional audit/migration tool; RAG remains optional navigation; GER becomes the bounded retry policy inside the delivery state machine; provider adapters remain replaceable plumbing.\",\n    \"needed\": true,\n    \"why_better\": \"This preserves the mechanisms that fixed real project failures—bounded workers, runtime feedback, clean Unity validation, repair limits, Git isolation, and human qualitative authority—while removing repeated identity/provenance transformations from the critical path. It also gives evolving canon a scoped impact model and makes merged player value, lead time, retries, and human effort measurable.\"\n  },\n  \"architecture_summary\": \"The architectural kernel is sound: durable repository state, bounded workers, deterministic supervision, Git isolation, runtime evidence, bounded repair, and human design/feel authority are appropriate for autonomous Unity development. The current composition is not sound enough as an autonomous system. Its implemented layers stop at task inspection, conformance bookkeeping, and read-only provider invocation; selection, write execution, Unity validation orchestration, recovery, readiness, and integration are disconnected or absent. It is also overbuilt relative to demonstrated gameplay throughput and models evolving canon with overly broad hashes plus undercomplete manual surface lists.\",\n  \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"No real gameplay task has traversed the proposed production path, and no production revalidation exists. Consequently, the largest unknown is the human and bookkeeping cost per repeated Unity feature—not whether individual schemas and adapters pass their isolated tests.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.92,\n  \"do_not_build_yet\": [\n    \"The full Progressive Decomposer platform or speculative full-game decomposition\",\n    \"Artifact Authority and Artifact GER before a concrete design artifact actually blocks delivery\",\n    \"A three-role, cross-provider ExecutionCrew before one write-capable worker can deliver a feature\",\n    \"Dependency-derived automatic readiness or dispatch authorization\",\n    \"GitHub Projects synchronization, automatic issue creation, or backlog replenishment\",\n    \"Parallel workers, resource-lock scheduling, merge queues, or automatic merge\",\n    \"More reconciliation auditors, streaming refiners, conflict arbiters, or global verification passes\",\n    \"A generalized project scanner beyond narrow checks encountered by the next feature\",\n    \"Provider fallback, model-comparison infrastructure, or additional provider hardening\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"At the frozen commit, the read-only commands `taskcontrol validate`, `taskcontrol ready`, and `taskcontrol state` showed a structurally valid 37-contract graph, unavailable readiness, zero dispatch authority, NSC-023 conformant, and NSC-003 not delivered. The working tree stayed clean.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:177\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The active runtime still lacks repository writes, approved command execution, production orchestration, task selection, GER integration, Unity execution, and Git integration.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md:48\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-003 claims to be a concrete single-agent contract while the current-state document separately lists seven unresolved input, steering, arrival, pathing, and restriction decisions. The contract is therefore not a safe autonomous handoff as written.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Tasks/NSC-003.yaml:10\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-003 and fourteen other contracts still contain evidence or locks for the deleted nested DoorPrototype scene. The shared resource registry also locks that obsolete path, yet `taskcontrol validate` passes because validation checks resource-key consistency rather than repository existence.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/RESOURCE_GROUPS.yaml:108\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The GDD still declares the deleted nested scene authoritative and calls `Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity` a noncanonical stub. The current implementation, scene-location check, and NSC-023 evidence instead identify `Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity` as authoritative.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:269\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Despite that semantic contradiction, NSC-023 derives as conformant because the evaluator checks the unchanged whole-GDD hash, current contract hash, and an explicitly enumerated surface list—not semantic agreement between those sources or completeness of the surface set.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:164\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The NSC-023 evidence record uses the new authoritative scene while its task contract's bootstrap evidence still names the obsolete scene, demonstrating internal authority drift immediately after the scene move.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/records/BASE-NSC-023-86af98f41ab5.json:31\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"A previous static validator passed the door implementation, but human Play Mode testing found that the progress bar did not visually update because the generated Filled Image lacked a sprite.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/AgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md:1\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The camera passed static evaluation twice but failed in Unity because it faced the wrong direction, did not follow the player, and later framed the door incorrectly. Structured human runtime feedback enabled repair.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback001.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The GER progress guard correctly detected a no-op refinement, but the subsequent pass added a null-target warning and regression test rather than further resolving framing. This shows that requiring any Assets delta can incentivize defensible-looking churn rather than outcome progress.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Assignment6GER/outputs/final_result.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Earlier builder tests invoked the production Build method, which opened and saved the canonical scene. The later in-memory seam and byte-preservation test corrected that unsafe validation design.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:15\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The GDD changed eleven times between August 13 and August 21 and mixes product mechanics with development-agent roles, pipeline coordination, current repository inventory, token policy, packages, and build configuration. A whole-document hash therefore has an unusually large blast radius.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Tracked production-pipeline source, tests, and supporting documents under Pipeline comprise roughly 28,000 lines excluding reconciliation outputs; committed reconciliation outputs add roughly 73,000 lines. The Unity gameplay, scene, and test text under Assets/NoSafeCircle is roughly 1,600 lines.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"From August 18 through the frozen August 23 commit, Git history is dominated by reconciliation, task/evidence, testing-policy, and provider-runtime work; Assets received scene/test safety changes but no new gameplay feature after Assignment 6.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/.git\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"gdd_iteration_concerns\": [\n    \"Current behavior is asymmetric and unsafe for iteration: any GDD edit makes every evidenced implementation stale because records bind the entire GDD hash, while planned but undelivered contracts remain unchanged unless a human notices and revises them. Implemented work is over-invalidated; planned work can silently remain stale.\",\n    \"The GDD should contain intended game behavior, not mutable repository inventory, provider workflow, token policy, or agent coordination. Those concerns currently make unrelated process edits look like gameplay-canon changes.\",\n    \"Stable requirement identifiers and explicit design-change events are missing. Task contracts cite prose sections, so there is no deterministic causal mapping from an edited decision to affected tasks, tests, assets, or evidence.\",\n    \"When design changes after planning, affected contracts should be revised or superseded before execution. When it changes after implementation, a reviewed impact proposal should open migration/revalidation work only for affected requirements and surfaces. Unaffected evidence should remain current.\",\n    \"Tuning and feel values should be allowed to evolve through playtesting without forcing global reconciliation. The system needs explicit classifications such as invariant, initial tuning value, implementation choice, and human-feel criterion.\",\n    \"The recent canonical scene relocation already demonstrates failed synchronization: implementation and evidence moved, while the GDD, task contracts, resource claims, build contract, and milestone documentation did not move together.\"\n  ],\n  \"human_intervention_points\": [\n    \"Choose product priority; dependency readiness cannot determine which feature creates the most player value.\",\n    \"Approve or clarify underspecified interaction semantics. NSC-003 currently needs decisions about input arbitration, click versus hold, destination replacement, arrival, pathing, and restriction lifetime.\",\n    \"Inspect Unity scenes, prefabs, serialized references, Console output, visual framing, UI readability, animation, input feel, and gameplay feel in Play Mode.\",\n    \"Decide whether a playtest finding is an implementation defect, a tuning change, or a canon/design revision.\",\n    \"Approve architecture changes, new design artifacts, scope changes, and newly discovered substantial prerequisites.\",\n    \"Review and merge changes while automatic integration remains disabled.\",\n    \"Provide or approve visual assets and judge art consistency; source-level tests cannot establish production presentation quality.\",\n    \"Until evidence publication is automated, select which test/runtime artifacts and conformance surfaces genuinely prove each gate.\"\n  ],\n  \"hypothetical_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Progressive Decomposition and Artifact Authority primarily protect against future distant-work ambiguity and unauthorized AI design. No artifact task or production artifact flow currently exists.\",\n    \"Parallel resource claims, multiple worktrees, merge queues, and GitHub synchronization protect against concurrency and visibility problems that a one-worker project has not reached.\",\n    \"Continuous backlog replenishment and autonomous task claiming protect a future mature delivery system; the current system cannot execute one selected ticket.\",\n    \"Provider interchangeability, capability classes, fallback, and extensive hostile JSON/container validation protect transport/vendor failures more than current game-development failures.\",\n    \"Repeated independent multi-model reconciliation verification protects global bootstrap completeness. It was useful for the one-time seed but is disproportionate for ordinary GDD edits or feature delivery.\",\n    \"A generalized Unity/code scanner and compact top-k context system mostly protect future context-scale concerns. The canonical GDD is only 349 lines, and current documentation already concedes top-k retrieval cannot prove complete canon.\",\n    \"Full Artifact GER, style evaluators, and automated artifact promotion protect future generated-content workflows, not the current blockers in movement, combat, enemies, rooms, and game flow.\"\n  ],\n  \"missing_components\": [\n    \"A restartable one-ticket delivery state machine\",\n    \"A production write-capable worker boundary with enforceable repository scope\",\n    \"Approved command execution for Git inspection and deterministic validation\",\n    \"ExecutionCrew or a thinner implementer/validator sequence connected to TaskExecution\",\n    \"Automatic measurement of actual changed paths and no-progress against unresolved gates\",\n    \"Unity host/Editor integration that emits XML, logs, exit status, tested commit/tree, and pre/post cleanliness\",\n    \"A single attempt journal joining task identity, invocation, worktree, commits, test evidence, human feedback, retries, and disposition\",\n    \"A reviewed dependency-readiness policy based on derived delivery state\",\n    \"A scoped GDD/design change-impact mechanism\",\n    \"A safe delivery/revalidation publisher tied to the integrated candidate commit\",\n    \"Recovery semantics for timeouts, partial writes, orphaned task requests, stale worktrees, failed validation, and interrupted human review\",\n    \"Throughput telemetry: lead time, model time/cost, repair count, human minutes, escaped defects, and merged player-value slices\"\n  ],\n  \"next_slice\": {\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"Human approval of a smaller replacement for NSC-003 covering the shared pointer/input foundation and first playable mouse-directed movement behavior, including exact input arbitration and arrival semantics\",\n      \"Correction of current canonical-scene references in the affected GDD inventory, task/resource records, and build requirement before execution\",\n      \"One isolated branch/worktree and one append-only attempt journal\",\n      \"One bounded write-capable implementation-agent run; no Planner, Artifact Authority, GitHub, or parallel scheduler\",\n      \"Contract-derived EditMode/component tests and PlayMode behavior tests using the non-mutating Unity policy\",\n      \"Deterministic changed-path, diff, Unity XML, exit-code, and clean-tree evidence\",\n      \"Human Play Mode assessment of click/hold behavior, UI arbitration, feel, framing, and Console state\",\n      \"At most two evidence-driven repair attempts, followed by merge or a concrete blocker report\",\n      \"A short measurement report covering elapsed time, human hands-on time, model attempts/cost, failures found, and infrastructure blockers\"\n    ],\n    \"proof_of_value\": \"A real, visible mouse-directed gameplay behavior must be merged from an isolated worktree within one working day, with no canonical-asset mutation, no unresolved completion gate, no more than two repair attempts, and approximately one hour or less of human hands-on work excluding ordinary playtesting. If the slice instead stalls on orchestration or evidence bookkeeping, that falsifies the near-term leverage claim and identifies the next minimal blocker.\",\n    \"title\": \"Feature-first delivery kernel: mouse-directed movement\",\n    \"why\": \"If additional infrastructure is forbidden, the first failure is the ambiguous NSC-003 contract and stale scene authority; immediately after that, the machinery has no authorized write/test/repair lane. Delivering a smaller movement slice exercises state, execution, Unity validation, human feedback, repair, and recovery in one transaction while producing actual player value.\"\n  },\n  \"observed_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Fresh bounded role invocations and explicit artifact handoffs successfully produced the original door prototype faster than a single long model context and made the static review trail inspectable.\",\n    \"Human Unity feedback plus bounded repair solved the visibly broken door progress indicator that static code/tests missed.\",\n    \"GER's structured runtime-feedback loop solved camera direction, follow, and framing failures that passed semantic/static evaluation.\",\n    \"Circuit-breaker accounting prevented unlimited camera repair attempts.\",\n    \"The no-op detector caught an actual Refiner response that claimed implementation without changing Assets, although its progress definition needs improvement.\",\n    \"The Unity testing policy, in-memory builder seam, committed-scene conformance tests, XML reporting, and clean-tree checks address the actual unsafe pattern where normal tests invoked a production scene-saving entry point.\",\n    \"Persistent task identities and contracts address repeated reconstruction and stale Assignment 5 goal artifacts across sessions.\",\n    \"The production GDD index's source hashes and deterministic rebuild address the actual stale July Assignment 4 knowledge base.\",\n    \"Separating builder capability from serialized scene conformance addresses an observed recurring error: code that can generate an asset is not proof that the committed asset contains it.\",\n    \"Human approval boundaries addressed real scope changes, such as the controls HUD amendment, without silently converting an agent suggestion into canon.\"\n  ],\n  \"overall_verdict\": \"partially_unsound\",\n  \"overengineering\": [\n    \"The repository built a large reconciliation/verification/refinement system for a one-time 37-record bootstrap, including domain workers, multiple auditor types, selective pass two, streaming field repairs, conflict clustering/arbitration, recovery, and extensive committed output history.\",\n    \"AgentRuntime heavily hardens recursive JSON values, subclass behavior, Windows path aliases, schemas, provider envelopes, usage metadata, atomic publication, and two transports before any production agent can edit gameplay or run Unity.\",\n    \"The 349-line GDD has a roughly 1,900-line production retrieval implementation and test suite, while workers are still expected to receive the whole GDD because retrieval completeness is unproven.\",\n    \"TaskGraph, TaskExecution, AgentRuntime, future ExecutionCrew, reconciliation, Git, Unity evidence, and conformance records each preserve overlapping identity/provenance without one transaction that connects them.\",\n    \"ArchitectureReview became the first real AgentRuntime consumer, proving transport but not the value-bearing risks of repository writes, Unity execution, repair, integration, or moving design.\",\n    \"The roadmap specifies Progressive Decomposition, Artifact Authority, GitHub mirroring, continuous scheduling, and parallelism before measuring one corrected task's delivery cost.\",\n    \"Documentation itself has become another mutable state layer: CURRENT_STATE says Codex migration is active near the top but later calls Codex unimplemented and repeats obsolete next-step instructions.\"\n  ],\n  \"state_and_authority_concerns\": [\n    \"Necessary boundaries are: approved product canon/design decisions; a versioned task/feature contract; integrated Git code/assets; deterministic validation artifacts; human qualitative approval; transient attempt state; and a derived scheduling view.\",\n    \"Task contracts should own intended work only. Git should own integrated implementation. One gate report should own validation evidence. One attempt journal should own transient execution/retry state. Readiness should be a deterministic policy view over those inputs.\",\n    \"Reconciliation snapshot, bootstrap provenance, contract hash, TaskExecution request, AgentRuntime request/result, future crew run, conformance record, and Git history currently repeat identity without an atomic transaction or recovery coordinator.\",\n    \"TaskExecution validates the supplied task path/hash syntactically but does not resolve and verify that identity against committed repository state before invoking AgentRuntime. It is an audit wrapper, not task authority.\",\n    \"The conformance evaluator trusts a manually authored surface list. A relevant omitted file can change without invalidation, while any unrelated GDD edit invalidates the record. This is undercomplete code impact combined with overbroad canon impact.\",\n    \"Invalid evidence is given global precedence for a task, but record immutability and correction/revocation semantics are not a complete operational recovery model.\",\n    \"The GDD currently combines game-design authority, development-process policy, implementation ownership, mutable repository inventory, and build configuration. These should not share one global canon hash.\",\n    \"The scene-location smoke test proves the actual Unity asset path but searches stale references only in Assets C# files. It therefore passes while the operational task/resource graph and GDD remain wrong.\",\n    \"The current `conformant` result proves internal identity/blob consistency for NSC-023, not semantic agreement across all claimed authorities or completeness of the change surface.\",\n    \"Mutable convenience views are acceptable for navigation, but START_HERE, CURRENT_STATE, master context, milestone records, individual ADRs, and DECISIONS currently disagree about active stage and implemented behavior, weakening repository-as-memory.\"\n  ],\n  \"strong_decisions\": [\n    \"A local deterministic process, rather than an LLM context, should own the long-running control loop.\",\n    \"Workers should receive one bounded, approved unit of work and should not rebuild the roadmap.\",\n    \"Agent claims about changed files, commands, tests, or success must not become deterministic evidence.\",\n    \"Runtime and human Unity observations are first-class feedback for interactive and visual work.\",\n    \"Repair attempts require budgets, circuit breakers, and escalation.\",\n    \"No-op attempts must consume budget; the progress measure should be improved to gate resolution rather than any diff.\",\n    \"Task definition, transient execution, integrated code, validation evidence, conformance, readiness, and human approval are correctly recognized as distinct concepts.\",\n    \"Reconciliation should propose reviewed changes rather than directly mutate the living graph.\",\n    \"Repository writing should occur on isolated branches/worktrees, with one worker before parallelism.\",\n    \"Missing design detection should be separate from authority to invent design.\",\n    \"Top-k retrieval is correctly treated as navigation rather than complete canon.\",\n    \"Autonomous dispatch is correctly fail-closed until a real delivery policy exists.\"\n  ],\n  \"structural_flaws\": [\n    \"The proposed hierarchy is not a closed control loop. Implemented code does not connect selected work to authorization, repository writes, Unity execution, repair, evidence publication, dependency readiness, merge, or the next task.\",\n    \"There is no canonical per-attempt aggregate. A future supervisor would need to coordinate several immutable artifact trees and infer which request, worktree, commits, validation, human feedback, and evidence record form one transaction.\",\n    \"Moving canon is treated as snapshot freshness rather than causal change. Whole-document hashing cannot distinguish an unrelated pipeline wording edit from a changed gameplay invariant.\",\n    \"The persistent graph already drifted after the canonical scene move, showing that neither reconciliation, graph validation, nor conformance currently maintains evolving authority.\",\n    \"Contracts marked `single_agent` are not necessarily executable: NSC-003 simultaneously carries that label and unresolved product decisions.\",\n    \"Current conformance can prove internally consistent evidence while task and GDD descriptions contradict the repository.\",\n    \"Evidence publication is manual and occurs after integration, so the path from candidate validation to durable conformance is neither atomic nor restartable.\",\n    \"Progress is not measured in merged player value, elapsed delivery time, human minutes, repair effectiveness, or escaped defects.\",\n    \"The architecture has separate abstractions for generation, evaluation, validation, review, and evidence, but no approved rule for selecting the minimum evaluator set by task risk.\",\n    \"If forbidden to add infrastructure for one slice, failure occurs first at contract ambiguity/stale authority, then at the absence of a write-capable execution and Unity-validation lane—not at missing RAG, decomposition, GitHub, or parallel scheduling.\"\n  ],\n  \"throughput_assessment\": \"Bounded agents have demonstrated useful local coding and repair leverage on the door and camera prototypes. The current production architecture has not demonstrated end-to-end leverage and is presently throughput-negative: five days of heavy pipeline work produced substantial auditability but no new gameplay feature, while readiness and authorization remain disabled. It cannot plausibly turn weeks into days until one feature can be selected, edited, validated in Unity, repaired, reviewed, and merged through a restartable path. Even then, humans remain the bottleneck for design ambiguity, visual assets, feel, balancing, and final integration, so the realistic target is faster implementation cycles—not unattended completion of the whole game.\",\n  \"verdict_reason\": \"The approach is not fundamentally wrong, but the current organization is partially unsound. Its strongest control principles are worth retaining; its critical path is fragmented, its evidence model does not handle evolving canon correctly, and its infrastructure investment is far ahead of demonstrated delivery. Reorganize around a minimal feature-delivery state machine, make scoped change impact explicit, and demote reconciliation, RAG, decomposition, artifact machinery, provider interchangeability, and continuous autonomy to optional services justified by observed failures.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/game_producer.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/game_producer.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"alternative_architecture\": {\n    \"description\": \"Reorganize around a thin, change-driven delivery controller. State: keep approved GDD requirements and immutable task-contract revisions in Git; keep one operational lifecycle ledger for planned/running/validating/blocked/review/done; treat the merge commit plus evidence manifest as delivery truth. Planning: human-prioritize one vertical slice and link it to stable requirement IDs; GDD diffs impact only linked work. Execution: one scoped writable coding worker on a branch, with checkpoint commits. Validation: deterministic Git checks, a real Windows Unity compile/EditMode/PlayMode/build adapter, one semantic diff review, and explicit human visual/feel gates. Recovery: reconcile attempt IDs against branches, commits, Unity artifacts, and merges so retries are idempotent. Unity integration: maintain one validated canonical scene registry and enforce project-lock/import-cache policy. Keep the existing GDDRAG, clean Unity runner, bounded runtime, and evidence concepts; demote global reconciliation to occasional audits.\",\n    \"needed\": true,\n    \"why_better\": \"It preserves the sound safety components while putting the missing delivery transaction at the center. It also treats GDD evolution as scoped change impact rather than globally invalidating evidence or rebuilding the roadmap.\"\n  },\n  \"architecture_summary\": \"The underlying principles are sound—bounded workers, deterministic facts, Git integration truth, runtime evidence, and human design authority—but the implemented architecture is not yet sound as an autonomous game-development investment. It is a heavily hardened read/review and evidence framework surrounding a nonexistent gameplay delivery loop. Worse, evolving-canon synchronization already fails: the canonical scene moved, yet the GDD, task contracts, and resource records still reference the deleted path while graph validation passes.\",\n  \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"The repository cannot reveal undocumented Unity-host automation or human workflows outside Git. If a reliable external execution/build lane already exists, the delivery gap is smaller, but it is not integrated with this architecture.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.94,\n  \"do_not_build_yet\": [\n    \"A general continuous supervisor or automatic dispatcher.\",\n    \"Parallel workers, claims, merge queues, and broad worktree scheduling.\",\n    \"Bidirectional GitHub Issues/Projects synchronization; choose one operational authority first.\",\n    \"Progressive Decomposer and Artifact Authority/Artifact GER until a real feature is blocked by missing approved design.\",\n    \"Provider fallback or additional provider/reviewer migrations.\",\n    \"Another global reconciliation or multi-model verification cycle for ordinary GDD edits.\",\n    \"Top-k-only compact task context; the current GDD is small enough to provide in full.\",\n    \"More conformance-schema hardening before a new gameplay delivery exercises the existing model.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"At the frozen commit, taskcontrol validation passes, but readiness is explicitly unavailable and authorization denies every task.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:202\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The conformance evaluator compares every record with a hash of the entire canonical GDD; any GDD edit makes otherwise unchanged evidence stale.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:164\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"OpenAI/Codex rejects repository writing and approved command execution, so the implemented provider path cannot advance gameplay.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/AgentRuntime/providers/openai_codex.py:89\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"TaskExecution only publishes task_request.json and delegates one generic invocation; it has no planning, Git, Unity, GER, evidence-publication, or completion behavior.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskExecution/task_runner.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The authoritative scene is now Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity, but numerous task contracts and resource locks still reference the deleted Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/RESOURCE_GROUPS.yaml:108\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-037 still says the current authoritative scene is the deleted path and incorrectly classifies the actual authoritative scene as a non-canonical stub.\",\n      \"path\": \"Tasks/NSC-037.yaml\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The current GDD itself still declares the deleted scene path canonical, demonstrating that a hash-current RAG can faithfully serve stale canon relative to implementation.\",\n      \"path\": \"Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:269\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The Windows build has no registered scenes.\",\n      \"path\": \"ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset:7\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Current gameplay movement still directly polls WASD and does not use the planned mouse-directed Input Actions path.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs:22\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Assignment 6 records two static-evaluator passes that remained unusable in Unity, followed by runtime-driven repairs and a no-op refinement rejection.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The clean Unity runner was created after a passing builder test destructively rewrote a tracked canonical scene.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/Testing/README.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"On the current HEAD ancestry after Assignment 6, 244 commits added about 115,877 lines under Pipeline/Docs/Tasks, while only three commits touched Assets/Packages/ProjectSettings—and those addressed test safety and scene relocation, not a new gameplay feature.\",\n      \"path\": \"git history 20c5228..9f2485d\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Eleven selected deterministic GDDRAG, TaskGraph, TaskExecution, AgentRuntime/provider, process-runner, and testing-policy smoke/regression suites passed against the frozen clean tree. No Unity test was executed in this review environment.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Production GDDRAG is hash-current and integrity-valid with 41 chunks, correctly solving stale-index detection but not incorrect human canon.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/GDDRAG/integrity.py\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"gdd_iteration_concerns\": [\n    \"A GDD edit updates neither existing task contracts nor resource references automatically. Rebuilding RAG only makes retrieval agree with the edited document.\",\n    \"Every delivered task currently binds the whole GDD hash, so an unrelated wording or design edit forces global revalidation rather than scoped impact analysis.\",\n    \"There is no operational state for an active attempt when canon changes: continue under its frozen contract, pause, supersede, or replan are human-only decisions with no implemented transaction.\",\n    \"The existing scene-path contradiction proves one-time bootstrap correctness does not provide ongoing synchronization.\",\n    \"Introduce stable requirement IDs and per-task requirement hashes. On a GDD change, classify linked planned work as needing replan and linked delivered work as needing targeted revalidation; leave unrelated work current.\",\n    \"Freeze the task-contract/canon revision for each active attempt. At a checkpoint, a human or explicit policy decides whether a relevant GDD change supersedes that attempt. Never silently retarget work in progress.\"\n  ],\n  \"human_intervention_points\": [\n    \"Choose product priority and approve task-contract clarifications. NSC-003 especially needs decisions on input arbitration, click/hold behavior, arrival/cancellation, and pathing.\",\n    \"Approve GDD changes and decide whether active work continues, is superseded, or needs remediation.\",\n    \"Operate or authorize the licensed Unity host and resolve project locks/import state.\",\n    \"Perform Play Mode feel, visual readability, camera framing, scene/prefab, sorting, and interaction checks.\",\n    \"Review and merge changes; retain this authority until several consecutive deliveries are reliable.\",\n    \"Currently, manually invoke a writable coding agent because the production adapters are read-only.\",\n    \"Currently, manually assemble/promote Unity results and conformance records after integration.\",\n    \"Acquire, approve, import, and visually integrate art/audio assets.\",\n    \"Worth automating next only after repeated use: Unity command/result capture, evidence-manifest generation, branch/attempt recovery, and requirement-impact alerts.\"\n  ],\n  \"hypothetical_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Parallel resource claims, multiple worktrees, merge queues, and GitHub synchronization protect a concurrency regime that does not exist yet.\",\n    \"Artifact Authority and Artifact GER protect future AI-authored design, but the current graph has no artifact task and no observed production blocker requiring it.\",\n    \"Progressive Decomposition addresses future coarse work; current execution fails before decomposition becomes the bottleneck.\",\n    \"Provider fallback and cross-provider review provenance do not advance a Unity ticket and are not yet production bottlenecks.\",\n    \"Much of AgentRuntime's adversarial JSON/subclass/path hardening protects theoretical provider-boundary attacks before providers have write authority.\",\n    \"The elaborate 15-auditor reconciliation/refinement/arbitration system protected a one-time bootstrap and should not become the routine response to GDD iteration.\",\n    \"Broad continuous-autonomy budgeting, backlog replenishment, and parallel scheduling are premature until one serial ticket can be delivered and recovered.\"\n  ],\n  \"missing_components\": [\n    \"One authoritative operational lifecycle ledger with attempt identity and explicit planned/running/validating/review/blocked/done/superseded states.\",\n    \"Evidence-derived dependency readiness tied to integrated delivery—not mutable task metadata.\",\n    \"A scoped writable implementer path with deterministic changed-file enforcement.\",\n    \"A real Unity-host execution adapter covering compilation, focused EditMode/PlayMode tests, and Windows build output.\",\n    \"Automatic evidence-manifest creation from actual Git and Unity results.\",\n    \"Branch/checkpoint/merge recovery that handles crashes and merged-but-unrecorded work idempotently.\",\n    \"Stable requirement-level GDD impact mapping.\",\n    \"Validation that task/resource/evidence paths still exist and agree with the current canonical scene registry.\",\n    \"Measured per-ticket elapsed time, human minutes, retries, token/cost use, and escaped-defect data.\"\n  ],\n  \"next_slice\": {\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"Human-freeze a narrow revision of NSC-005, resolving its duplicated/readability gates and specifying the smallest visible denied-mana feedback without expanding spell design.\",\n      \"Implement PlayerMana reset and denied-spend signaling plus the existing PlayerManaUI feedback consumer.\",\n      \"Extend PlayerMana Play Mode tests for reset, regeneration-delay reset, and denied-spend signaling.\",\n      \"Run the focused clean Unity PlayMode suite on the real host and perform one human readability check.\",\n      \"Merge through an ordinary branch and create one minimal delivery/evidence manifest using the existing mechanisms.\",\n      \"Record elapsed time, agent time, human minutes, retries, Unity import/test time, and manual edits.\"\n    ],\n    \"proof_of_value\": \"From a clean checkout, deliver a visibly improved, tested, merged gameplay capability within one working day, with no repository mutation from tests and no more than roughly 60–90 minutes of human orchestration. Repeatability—not another schema—is the first credible evidence that weeks can become days.\",\n    \"title\": \"Actual game production: deliver NSC-005 end to end\",\n    \"why\": \"NSC-005 extends an existing small component and existing Play Mode suite, avoids NSC-003's unresolved input design, and isolates the real delivery bottleneck. Use an ordinary writable coding-agent workflow and the existing clean test/evidence pieces; do not build a supervisor first.\"\n  },\n  \"observed_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Repository-resident canon, contracts, logs, and decisions address real context/session loss and stale chat memory.\",\n    \"Production GDDRAG freshness/integrity addresses the actually stale July Assignment 4 index.\",\n    \"Reconciliation addressed stale Assignment 5 planning and found substantial semantic gaps before the initial graph was persisted.\",\n    \"GER runtime feedback solved camera defects missed by static evaluation.\",\n    \"The no-op progress guard and circuit breaker solve the observed refiner that claimed implementation without changing Assets.\",\n    \"The Unity testing policy and clean runner solve the observed passing test that rewrote a canonical scene.\",\n    \"Committed-scene conformance tests distinguish builder capability from actual serialized state, an observed source of false confidence.\",\n    \"Immutable run history and bounded recovery address timeouts/failures experienced while producing the reconciliation bootstrap.\"\n  ],\n  \"overall_verdict\": \"partially_unsound\",\n  \"overengineering\": [\n    \"Approximately 23,059 lines of executable Python/PowerShell across the production pipeline areas versus about 1,508 lines of C# under Assets/NoSafeCircle, excluding generated reconciliation outputs.\",\n    \"A 15-auditor reconciliation pass, streaming field repairs, conflict arbitration, and extensive recovery machinery for a 37-contract one-time bootstrap.\",\n    \"Provider-neutral runtime and two live adapters were proven through ArchitectureReview rather than a writable gameplay ticket.\",\n    \"Strict immutable invocation publication and deep hostile-value validation are disproportionate before any provider can write or execute approved commands.\",\n    \"Thirty-seven detailed contracts were produced before the project had a current lifecycle/readiness model, and their embedded repository evidence is already stale.\",\n    \"Multiple long context documents retain superseded milestone/status semantics, increasing maintenance and authority ambiguity.\",\n    \"Whole-GDD conformance invalidation creates expensive revalidation work instead of precise safety.\"\n  ],\n  \"state_and_authority_concerns\": [\n    \"Necessary boundaries: human-approved design canon; immutable task scope/revision; integrated Git tree; deterministic Unity/Git evidence; worker claims; and explicit human visual/design approval.\",\n    \"The AgentRuntime/TaskExecution separation is appropriate: generic invocation should not fabricate NSC task identity.\",\n    \"Tasks correctly should define work rather than completion, but removing status without introducing an operational lifecycle authority leaves the graph inert.\",\n    \"Reconciliation snapshots, bootstrap repository observations, task-embedded GDD excerpts, RAG chunks, and current-state documents duplicate facts and can disagree; the scene-path drift demonstrates this.\",\n    \"Git should remain implementation authority, but delivery needs a single evidence manifest and materialized lifecycle state rather than several indirect records.\",\n    \"Do not add GitHub as a second mutable authority. If added, make it a one-way projection of the local lifecycle ledger.\",\n    \"Human approval is currently correctly retained for interactive quality, but manually recording approval inside hand-authored evidence JSON is costly and error-prone.\"\n  ],\n  \"strong_decisions\": [\n    \"Use deterministic code for identity, hashes, graph structure, Git facts, test parsing, and safety checks.\",\n    \"Treat provider/agent output as claims rather than completion evidence.\",\n    \"Keep workers bounded and begin with one serial worker.\",\n    \"Make Unity/runtime evidence capable of overriding static semantic approval.\",\n    \"Fail tests when they mutate the repository, even if assertions pass.\",\n    \"Separate builder-generation tests from committed-scene conformance tests.\",\n    \"Keep reconciliation snapshots immutable and prevent them from directly rewriting living work.\",\n    \"Fail closed on stale RAG indexes while acknowledging retrieval is not complete canon.\",\n    \"Keep final design, visual, play-feel, and merge authority human-owned for now.\"\n  ],\n  \"structural_flaws\": [\n    \"There is no end-to-end state transition from selected contract to writable execution, Unity validation, merge, evidence publication, and dependent readiness.\",\n    \"The first break without more infrastructure is immediate: taskcontrol will not produce a ready task and both providers reject writes/commands. If a human bypasses that boundary, task/canon ambiguity and drift break correctness next.\",\n    \"Schema-v2 removed completion state but no lifecycle authority replaced it, so the dependency graph cannot schedule anything.\",\n    \"Moving-target synchronization is not implemented; a clean structural validation passes despite deleted canonical paths throughout the graph and GDD.\",\n    \"Conformance uses a global GDD hash instead of task-relevant requirement identities, making routine iteration globally expensive.\",\n    \"Graph validation checks syntax and relationships but not whether referenced Unity paths or repository evidence remain current.\",\n    \"The production runtime has been proven on architecture review, not implementation, repair, Unity, or recovery of a gameplay ticket.\",\n    \"The Unity lane is a human-invoked Windows PowerShell wrapper, not an integrated execution/recovery service; Windows build settings are still empty.\",\n    \"Several supposedly single-agent contracts assert design completeness that the repository's own current-state document later disputes, particularly NSC-003.\",\n    \"Canonical architecture/status documentation contradicts itself about readiness, milestone order, and scene authority.\",\n    \"No throughput telemetry establishes that the additional infrastructure reduces elapsed time or human effort.\"\n  ],\n  \"throughput_assessment\": \"The current system cannot turn weeks into days because it cannot autonomously start or deliver a gameplay task. Its strongest production proof is one camera feature whose static checks passed twice while Unity remained unusable, requiring two human runtime failures and three refinements. Since that work, HEAD ancestry contains 244 commits and roughly 115,877 added lines under Pipeline/Docs/Tasks but no new gameplay feature. The architecture may eventually reduce rediscovery, unsafe testing, and recovery cost, but major leverage requires repeated end-to-end delivery. A credible threshold is two or three consecutive small gameplay tickets delivered in a day each with bounded retries and under about 60–90 minutes of human orchestration per ticket.\",\n  \"verdict_reason\": \"Keep the bounded-worker, deterministic-validation, RAG freshness, Unity safety, and evidence ideas. Reject the current investment sequence and moving-canon model. The project has optimized review, provenance, and hypothetical autonomy risks before implementing the one transaction that creates value: safely turning an approved gameplay change into a tested, playable, merged increment.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/game_technical_director.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/game_technical_director.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"alternative_architecture\": {\n    \"description\": \"Replace the milestone stack with a thin delivery-first loop. State: keep the GDD, Git main, and small versioned contracts only for active/near-term slices; derive operational status from persisted run state, Git/PR state, and validation manifests. Planning: a human selects the next vertical slice and approves its behavior; an LLM produces a bounded contract plus GDD-section impact map. Execution: a local driver creates one branch/worktree and invokes one write-capable worker, followed by a fresh reviewer. Validation: run deterministic checks and Unity compile/EditMode/PlayMode tests through the clean runner, then require human checks for feel and visuals. Recovery: persist a small state machine—planned, implementing, validating, needs-repair, needs-human, merged—with commits, evidence paths, and bounded retry counts. GDD changes: map changed sections or stable requirement IDs only to affected contracts, classifying them as unaffected, contract-review, revalidation, or rework; never globally rebuild the backlog by default. Unity integration: the Windows Unity host owns engine execution, outputs XML/logs outside the repository, checks tree cleanliness, and distinguishes in-memory builders from committed scene/prefab conformance.\",\n    \"needed\": true,\n    \"why_better\": \"It retains the safety mechanisms supported by actual failures while making gameplay delivery the primary proof. It eliminates several authority handoffs, handles an iterative GDD causally instead of through a whole-document hash, and can produce useful game progress before scheduling, artifact generation, or parallelism exist.\"\n  },\n  \"architecture_summary\": \"The repository contains four substantial production foundations: a 37-contract TaskGraph, validated GDD retrieval, evidence-derived conformance, and provider-neutral read/review invocation. It does not contain the production path that creates throughput: no enabled readiness, dispatch authorization, write-capable gameplay worker, execution crew, Git lifecycle, Unity execution, merge loop, or completion propagation. The result is a sophisticated safety and audit shell around a small Unity prototype, not yet an autonomous game-development system.\",\n  \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"The frozen repository cannot show whether a separate development machine can execute reliable automated Unity runs for new work. It records credible prior Unity 6000.1.8f1 results, but no new production task has traversed the proposed end-to-end loop.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.94,\n  \"do_not_build_yet\": [\n    \"Continuous scheduling and backlog replenishment\",\n    \"Parallel workers, generalized leases, and resource-lock scheduling\",\n    \"GitHub Issues/Projects synchronization\",\n    \"Automatic merge or provider fallback\",\n    \"The bundled Progressive Decomposer platform\",\n    \"Artifact Authority and Artifact GER until a real design artifact blocks delivery\",\n    \"More provider adapters or ArchitectureReview plumbing\",\n    \"Top-k-only context packs presented as complete canon\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Successful read-only diagnostics at the frozen commit reported 37 valid schema-v2 contracts, an acyclic graph, disabled autonomous dispatch, unavailable readiness, zero authorized tasks, and a clean working tree before and after.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-023 derives as conformant from its committed baseline, demonstrating that the evidence evaluator works for one historical feature.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/records/BASE-NSC-023-86af98f41ab5.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The production GDD index validated as CURRENT with 41 chunks and matching source hash, but documentation says the whole GDD must still be supplied because retrieval coverage is unproven.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/GDDRAG\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"AgentRuntime explicitly lacks repository writing, approved command execution, gameplay roles, Unity, GER, task selection, Git workflow, readiness, and authorization.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/AgentRuntime/README.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"TaskExecution is currently only an immutable task-identity wrapper around one generic invocation.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskExecution/task_runner.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-003 declares itself concrete and single-agent, while current-state guidance identifies unresolved input arbitration, steering, cancellation, arrival, pathing, and restriction semantics.\",\n      \"path\": \"Tasks/NSC-003.yaml\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The GDD still names Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity as canonical, while implementation and evidence moved authority to Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity.\",\n      \"path\": \"Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The conformance evaluator compares every evidence record against the hash of the entire current GDD, so any GDD text change makes otherwise unchanged evidence stale.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The camera GER passed static review twice while remaining unusable in Unity, then encountered a no-op refinement before succeeding through runtime feedback and bounded retries.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Earlier builder tests called the production scene-saving entry point; later commits added an in-memory seam, direct committed-scene tests, and byte-preservation checks.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The actual project still has direct hardware polling for WASD and door interaction, no spells or enemy runtime, missing required navigation packages, and no registered build scene.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Across the 244 commits after the camera feature commit, 136 touched Pipeline, only three touched Assets/NoSafeCircle, and none touched runtime gameplay scripts; those three game-area changes were scene/test safety work rather than a new mechanic.\",\n      \"path\": \"Git history: 20c5228..9f2485d\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Pipeline areas contain roughly 13,000 nonblank lines across TaskGraph, GDDRAG, AgentRuntime, and TaskExecution, versus roughly 1,448 under Assets/NoSafeCircle; Reconciliation adds about 84,000 nonblank lines including generated history.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"CURRENT_STATE contains contradictory lifecycle claims: its opening describes Stage 4C/Codex migration as implemented, while its later Immediate Next Goal says Claude migration is next and Codex remains unimplemented.\",\n      \"path\": \"Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"gdd_iteration_concerns\": [\n    \"The present whole-GDD hash treats an unrelated wording, art, balance, or repository-description edit as grounds to revalidate every delivered task.\",\n    \"Contracts copy substantial GDD prose and bootstrap repository observations, creating a second version of design/current-state facts that can drift independently.\",\n    \"Stable requirement IDs and an implemented requirement-to-task impact map do not exist.\",\n    \"The promised reconciliation-to-graph diff/review/apply path is not implemented, so immutable snapshots do not synchronize the living graph.\",\n    \"The scene relocation demonstrates the problem now: canon, contract bootstrap evidence, current implementation, and conformance evidence disagree on the canonical path while NSC-023 still derives as conformant.\",\n    \"When canon changes, the correct flow should be scoped impact review: identify affected requirements, amend or supersede only affected contracts, decide revalidation versus rework, preserve prior delivery evidence as lineage, and deterministically recalculate affected dependencies.\",\n    \"Full reconciliation should be reserved for major scope changes, milestone audits, or evidence that the graph has materially diverged—not normal iterative GDD editing.\"\n  ],\n  \"human_intervention_points\": [\n    \"Approve gameplay semantics where the GDD leaves feel, arbitration, cancellation, navigation, or tuning ambiguous.\",\n    \"Play the game to judge movement feel, camera framing, readability, animation, encounter pacing, balance, and fun.\",\n    \"Inspect scene, prefab, package, Input Actions, and build-configuration changes before merge.\",\n    \"Resolve architectural changes and cross-system ownership conflicts discovered during implementation.\",\n    \"Approve canon amendments, contract supersession, and generated design/content.\",\n    \"Review and merge changes until the single-ticket lane has a strong production track record.\",\n    \"Perform final Windows build, device/input, performance, and presentation validation.\"\n  ],\n  \"hypothetical_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Parallel resource locks and generalized claims protect against multi-worker conflicts before one production worker exists.\",\n    \"GitHub dashboard synchronization addresses visibility at a scale the project has not reached.\",\n    \"Automatic provider fallback protects availability but risks changing behavior and is not an observed delivery blocker.\",\n    \"Artifact Authority, artifact promotion, and multi-evaluator Artifact GER address future generated-design work; the current bootstrap contains zero artifact tasks.\",\n    \"Continuous scheduling, backlog replenishment, merge queues, and daily budget governance protect later unattended scale.\",\n    \"Most adversarial immutable-evidence ancestry and ambiguity cases are prudent hardening but have not materially accelerated the one existing production baseline.\",\n    \"Top-k context optimization addresses future token cost, while the current policy still sends the entire relatively small GDD.\"\n  ],\n  \"missing_components\": [\n    \"An approved dependency-readiness rule derived from current conformance\",\n    \"A write-capable, scope-bounded implementation invocation\",\n    \"A minimal branch/worktree execution driver\",\n    \"Task-specific Unity compile, EditMode, PlayMode, and committed-artifact validation\",\n    \"A small persisted recovery state machine around one delivery attempt\",\n    \"A deterministic diff between evolving canon, task contracts, and delivered surfaces\",\n    \"A reviewed mechanism for amending or superseding affected contracts\",\n    \"A proven context builder that cannot omit cross-cutting canon\",\n    \"Actual gameplay integration through review, merge, evidence publication, and downstream readiness\"\n  ],\n  \"next_slice\": {\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"A short human-approved decision note resolving primary click, held-pointer steering, UI click suppression, command replacement/cancellation, arrival behavior, and prototype pathing expectations\",\n      \"Input Actions-backed pointer position and primary-click actions\",\n      \"PlayerMovement-owned world-space pointer projection and destination/hold steering\",\n      \"Owner-controlled movement restriction, suspend/re-enable, and restart interfaces\",\n      \"Integration with door selection so one click can request approach without independent pointer projection\",\n      \"Focused component and Play Mode tests using non-saving seams\",\n      \"A clean-tree Unity validation run with external XML/logs\",\n      \"Human Play Mode approval for movement feel, camera framing, UI arbitration, and door approach\",\n      \"One reviewed merge plus minimal delivery manifest sufficient to derive task conformance and unlock dependencies\"\n    ],\n    \"proof_of_value\": \"Starting from one approved contract, a bounded worker creates a gameplay diff, survives deterministic and Unity validation, incorporates runtime feedback through at most a small fixed repair budget, receives human feel approval, merges, and causes dependent work to become ready. That is the first credible demonstration of days-versus-weeks leverage.\",\n    \"title\": \"Playable mouse movement and door-approach delivery lane\",\n    \"why\": \"Movement is foundational to spells, doors, pursuit, restart, and victory suspension. It also exercises every currently unproven production boundary: design clarification, Input Actions, write execution, canonical-scene integration, runtime validation, repair, merge, conformance, and dependency propagation. Build only the narrow glue needed for this slice.\"\n  },\n  \"observed_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Repository-backed state prevents loss of context between model sessions.\",\n    \"Bounded contracts reduced scope drift in the door, mana, and camera coursework runs.\",\n    \"Runtime evidence re-entering GER fixed camera defects that semantic/static review approved incorrectly.\",\n    \"No-op change detection prevented a refiner from claiming progress without modifying gameplay files.\",\n    \"Circuit breakers bounded repeated camera repair.\",\n    \"The Unity testing policy, clean runner, in-memory builder seam, and committed-scene checks directly address tests that previously saved canonical assets.\",\n    \"Scene identity/location checks address duplicate or ambiguous DoorPrototype scene authority.\",\n    \"Immutable reconciliation outputs and resume utilities address real reconciliation timeout, recovery, identity, and merge-conflict problems—although those are pipeline-self-management wins rather than game throughput.\",\n    \"Current production GDDRAG addresses the observed staleness of the older July Assignment 4 index.\",\n    \"Line-ending normalization addresses the repository's Windows/Linux validation boundary.\"\n  ],\n  \"overall_verdict\": \"partially_unsound\",\n  \"overengineering\": [\n    \"A multi-model, domain-parallel reconciliation and verification system was built before one production gameplay ticket could execute.\",\n    \"The one-time 37-contract bootstrap required extensive schema, refinement, recovery, finding-identity, and arbitration machinery disproportionate to this small game.\",\n    \"TaskGraph completion was replaced by a task-specific immutable evidence/conformance system before replacement readiness shipped, leaving the graph inert.\",\n    \"AgentRuntime has extensive hostile-value, schema, path, transport, and publication hardening while its providers remain incapable of implementing gameplay.\",\n    \"ArchitectureReview was chosen as the first production runtime consumer, proving review transport rather than game delivery.\",\n    \"Task contract, evidence record, conformance result, readiness, authorization, runtime request, task-execution request, reconciliation snapshot, and future GitHub state create too many handoffs for a single-developer capstone.\",\n    \"Large generated reconciliation history is stored in the source repository even though only the approved current contract delta is needed for day-to-day development.\",\n    \"Milestone plans include artifact generation, supervisor, continuous autonomy, and parallelism before a single corrected delivery path exists.\"\n  ],\n  \"state_and_authority_concerns\": [\n    \"Necessary authorities are: approved GDD/design decisions for intent; task contract for bounded scope; integrated Git tree for implementation; deterministic/Unity/human validation for acceptance; and human merge/canon approval.\",\n    \"It is useful to distinguish a provider claim from test evidence and current conformance from dispatch permission, but those distinctions do not each require a heavyweight independent subsystem.\",\n    \"Reconciliation snapshots are valid audit history, but they duplicate responsibility when treated as a standing operational layer alongside Tasks and current conformance.\",\n    \"Task contracts should not retain mutable repository-state observations as if they remain current facts.\",\n    \"AgentRuntime versus TaskExecution is a reasonable generic/task-specific boundary, but it currently adds audit ceremony without an execution consumer.\",\n    \"Operational run state should live in one restartable supervisor record or PR/check state, not be recreated across task files, evidence records, runtime artifacts, future GitHub fields, and status documents.\",\n    \"Git commit/PR identity plus a compact validation manifest can replace much of the bespoke evidence DAG for ordinary tasks; richer lineage is warranted only for revalidation or regulated/high-risk surfaces.\",\n    \"Human authority is currently correctly retained, but fail-closed authorization has become a permanent stop rather than a guard around an enabled narrow path.\"\n  ],\n  \"strong_decisions\": [\n    \"A deterministic local process—not an LLM context—should own continuity, budgets, retries, and recovery.\",\n    \"Workers should receive one bounded task and should not reconstruct the global roadmap.\",\n    \"Git and Unity evidence must outrank worker claims.\",\n    \"Implementation should be isolated from main, and merged/integrated state should define delivery.\",\n    \"Runtime, visual, and interaction evidence must be able to overturn static approval.\",\n    \"Canonical Unity scenes and prefabs must not be mutated by normal tests.\",\n    \"Missing design should be escalated rather than silently invented.\",\n    \"No-op repairs must consume retry budget.\",\n    \"Start with one worker; add parallelism only after reliable delivery.\",\n    \"Reconciliation should propose reviewed changes rather than directly rewriting operational state.\",\n    \"Design completeness and execution size are legitimately different questions.\",\n    \"Provider-neutral generic invocation is useful when kept below game-specific task semantics.\"\n  ],\n  \"structural_flaws\": [\n    \"The implemented architecture ends before the activities that produce game value: selecting, authorizing, writing, validating in Unity, merging, and unlocking dependent work.\",\n    \"Schema-v2 removed task completion status and deliberately disabled readiness before evidence-based dependency readiness was implemented.\",\n    \"The moving-GDD model is global and hash-based rather than scoped and causal.\",\n    \"The bootstrap classified NSC-003 as concrete/single-agent even though the repository's own current-state document now requires multiple human design decisions before implementation.\",\n    \"Conformance verifies record consistency and selected surfaces, not the semantic truth of every repository observation copied into a contract or GDD.\",\n    \"Documentation is not a reliable current-state control plane because the same file contains incompatible next-step and implementation claims.\",\n    \"The architecture plans a generic scanner, context builder, decomposer, artifact authority, supervisor, and execution crew as separate milestones instead of proving the smallest vertical delivery path.\",\n    \"The provider boundary assumes externally enforced read-only containment for repository review; no equivalent approved containment/write policy exists for gameplay implementation.\",\n    \"The current canonical scene is still a generated monolithic integration surface, so many nominally independent tasks will contend on the builder and scene until prefab/runtime composition boundaries improve.\",\n    \"The project has optimized correctness metadata while the required game remains at door/mana/camera prototype maturity with no spells, enemies, navigation foundation, restart, victory, or build scene registration.\"\n  ],\n  \"throughput_assessment\": \"Major near-term leverage is not currently plausible. The simpler historical crews did produce a door prototype, mana system, and camera feature, showing that bounded AI implementation can help. The production rewrite has not reproduced that result. Since the camera feature, the frozen ancestry contains 244 commits, 136 touching Pipeline, three touching the game area, and zero touching runtime gameplay scripts. Today the machinery can validate its graph, retrieve canon, inspect one conformance baseline, and run bounded read-only reviews; it cannot advance a gameplay task. If forbidden to build infrastructure for one slice, the first failure is immediate: taskcontrol cannot select work, authorization is denied, and AgentRuntime cannot write or run Unity. Progress would require manually bypassing the production machinery and using a human-driven coding/Unity workflow.\",\n  \"verdict_reason\": \"The architecture's governing principles are sound and worth retaining, but its production partitioning, milestone order, and GDD synchronization model are not. Safety infrastructure has displaced game development, the operational graph was made inert, and evolving canon is handled with coarse invalidation rather than reviewed impact. Reorganize around one thin write-test-review-merge gameplay lane; only add infrastructure when that lane exposes a repeated bottleneck.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/llm_reliability_engineer.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/llm_reliability_engineer.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"alternative_architecture\": {\n    \"description\": \"Use a thin feature-delivery kernel. State: separate stable product requirements from engineering policy and generated repository inventory; bind feature contracts only to relevant requirement IDs. Planning: select and clarify one near-term vertical slice, with scoped impact analysis for later canon changes instead of global reconciliation. Execution: one write-capable agent in an isolated branch/worktree, using externally enforced file and command permissions. Validation: Git/scope checks, clean Unity Edit/Play Mode tests, one fresh semantic diff review, then targeted human experiential review. Recovery: preserve the branch and attempt journal, feed specific failure evidence into at most two repairs, then escalate. Unity integration: explicitly distinguish in-memory builders from committed scenes/prefabs and require non-mutating validation. Add deterministic scheduling only after several successful deliveries.\",\n    \"needed\": true,\n    \"why_better\": \"It preserves the strongest current safety boundaries while removing global verifier swarms, duplicated state, prompt anchoring, and provider infrastructure from the gameplay critical path.\"\n  },\n  \"architecture_summary\": \"The architecture contains sound safety primitives but is not sound enough, as currently organized and implemented, for autonomous Unity delivery. It is assurance-heavy and delivery-light: canon retrieval, graph validation, evidence modeling, reconciliation, and provider transport exist, but no production path can write a gameplay change, invoke Unity, perform GER, derive readiness, dispatch, or merge. Its moving-canon model is especially weak because product design, engineering policy, and dated repository facts are mixed together and synchronized through duplicated artifacts.\",\n  \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"Actual model spend is not normalized or retained comparably. Cost severity is inferred from prompt sizes, worker counts, turn limits, retries, and history rather than invoices. Feature throughput has also never been measured through a complete production delivery.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.92,\n  \"do_not_build_yet\": [\n    \"Continuous supervisor or autonomous scheduler\",\n    \"Parallel workers and worktree pools\",\n    \"GitHub Issues/Projects synchronization\",\n    \"Progressive Decomposer and Artifact Authority platform\",\n    \"More provider migrations or cross-provider comparison infrastructure\",\n    \"Another full-project reconciliation run\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"At the frozen clean HEAD, taskcontrol validates 37 contracts, but readiness is explicitly unavailable and zero tasks are authorized for dispatch.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The authoritative scene moved to Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity in commit 484e66a, but 16 task contracts, RESOURCE_GROUPS, PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS, and GDD canon still reference the deleted nested scene. Structural task validation nevertheless passes.\",\n      \"path\": \"Tasks/; Pipeline/TaskGraph/RESOURCE_GROUPS.yaml; Pipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml; Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Current conformance hashes the entire GDD and marks evidence stale when that hash changes, regardless of whether the edit affects the task.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:164\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The production reconciliation prompt suite totals 4,251 lines and contains dated closure sections prescribing conclusions repaired during earlier rounds.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/Reconciliation/prompts/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Nine reconciliation workers receive prior routing hints and the same complete key registry. Verifier diversity defaults to Claude opus and sonnet aliases, preserving provider-family and prompt correlation.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/Reconciliation/parallel_reconciliation_agent.py:617; Pipeline/Reconciliation/verification_crew.py:71\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Parallel verification defines nine domain coverage audits plus six evidence/structure/execution audits before refiners and selective re-verification.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/Reconciliation/parallel_verification_crew.py:196\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Production pipeline Python totals approximately 24,623 lines, compared with approximately 1,508 lines of Unity C# game code.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/; Assets/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"History contains 56 commits touching Reconciliation, 41 touching TaskGraph, and 21 touching AgentRuntime/TaskExecution/ArchitectureReview, versus 7 commits touching Assets.\",\n      \"path\": \"Git history through 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Assignment 6 records two static evaluator passes followed by two human Unity runtime failures, then a refiner claim that made no Assets changes.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:90\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"CURRENT_STATE first says the Codex ArchitectureReview migration is implemented, then later says Claude migration is next and Codex remains unimplemented.\",\n      \"path\": \"Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md:48; Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md:631\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Read-only checks executed successfully: taskcontrol validate, taskcontrol ready, NSC-023 state, and GDDRAG validate. HEAD/tree and the worktree remained unchanged; NSC-023 is the sole demonstrated conformant production task.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"gdd_iteration_concerns\": [\n    \"The root GDD mixes gameplay intent, agent workflow policy, package choices, build configuration, and dated repository inventory. An engineering change can therefore appear to be a game-design change.\",\n    \"Whole-document hashing creates global revalidation pressure from unrelated canon edits.\",\n    \"Sequential RAG chunk IDs are reproducible but are not durable requirement identities across heading insertion or reordering.\",\n    \"Task contracts copy GDD prose and bootstrap repository observations, creating stale replicas instead of durable references.\",\n    \"No demonstrated workflow maps a changed clause to only the affected contracts, code/assets, tests, and evidence.\",\n    \"The scene relocation proves synchronization is not transactional: production code, tests, and evidence moved while canon, resource claims, and task evidence did not.\",\n    \"Routine GDD evolution needs a reviewed requirement-level impact workflow. Full reconciliation should be an exceptional audit, not the normal synchronization mechanism.\"\n  ],\n  \"human_intervention_points\": [\n    \"Approve ambiguous gameplay mechanics and input arbitration. NSC-003 currently lacks enough decisions for a safe handoff.\",\n    \"Approve genuine canon or architecture changes rather than letting an agent infer design.\",\n    \"Perform Play Mode, input-feel, framing, readability, animation, visual-quality, and balance judgments.\",\n    \"Inspect scene, prefab, package, and build-setting changes where serialization or editor behavior matters.\",\n    \"Review and merge initially; merge automation should wait for repeated delivery evidence.\",\n    \"Decide whether a circuit-breaker failure requires repair, task rescoping, contract change, or design change.\"\n  ],\n  \"hypothetical_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"AgentRuntime exact-type, polymorphic-container, Windows device-name, deep-JSON, and immutable-publication hardening protects mostly adversarial transport cases not experienced during game delivery.\",\n    \"Evidence-record cycle, incomparable-ancestry, and ambiguity logic is covered synthetically but has no real production revalidation history.\",\n    \"GitHub mirroring, leases, merge queues, and parallel resource scheduling address future scale, not the current single-worker bottleneck.\",\n    \"Artifact Authority and style GER address future generated design/content; the current graph has zero artifact tasks.\",\n    \"Eight-role ArchitectureReview provider provenance and future cross-provider comparison do not advance gameplay delivery.\",\n    \"Automatic backlog replenishment and continuous supervision protect a future autonomous queue that does not yet exist.\"\n  ],\n  \"missing_components\": [\n    \"A production end-to-end gameplay lane from approved contract through implementation, Unity validation, review, merge, and evidence.\",\n    \"Stable requirement/clause identities and scoped GDD-change impact links.\",\n    \"A write-capable worker boundary and externally enforced approved command execution.\",\n    \"Production ExecutionCrew/GER integration, or a substantially thinner equivalent.\",\n    \"Restartable attempt/lease state for one real task.\",\n    \"Cross-representation referential validation covering canon-described paths, task resource claims, builders, tests, and actual Unity assets.\",\n    \"Automated compact delivery/revalidation receipt creation.\",\n    \"Per-feature token, latency, retry, defect, and human-minute telemetry.\",\n    \"A real production revalidation example; only NSC-023 has a baseline.\"\n  ],\n  \"next_slice\": {\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"Obtain a one-page human-approved input decision covering click/hold semantics, UI/gameplay arbitration, destination replacement and cancellation, arrival tolerance, and pathing expectations.\",\n      \"Supersede or split NSC-003. Make the first contract cover shared pointer projection plus visible click-to-destination/hold-steer movement; defer restriction/reset/suspend APIs unless required by the implementation seam.\",\n      \"Correct the authoritative scene path across GDD engineering facts, task/resource claims, and project requirements, then add one deterministic cross-representation path check.\",\n      \"Implement the movement slice in one isolated branch using Input Actions, shared gameplay-plane projection, scene-builder wiring, and the committed Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity artifact.\",\n      \"Run clean targeted Edit/Play Mode validation, obtain human movement-feel approval, perform one fresh semantic diff review, merge, and publish one compact delivery receipt.\"\n    ],\n    \"proof_of_value\": \"Within one working day, an approved brief becomes merged, visibly playable mouse-directed movement with clean Unity evidence, bounded model usage, no global reconciliation, and no manual repair of duplicated operational state. Record elapsed time, model calls/tokens where available, repair count, escaped defects, and human minutes.\",\n    \"title\": \"NSC-003A mouse movement vertical slice through a thin delivery lane\",\n    \"why\": \"It is player-visible, unlocks Fireball, Frost Field, and door targeting, and tests the actual value proposition more directly than another infrastructure milestone.\"\n  },\n  \"observed_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Runtime evidence re-entering GER directly addresses the camera’s two static-pass/runtime-fail incidents.\",\n    \"No-op detection and circuit breakers address the observed refiner claim that made no Assets change. Progress should, however, be tied to resolving a named failure rather than merely producing any diff.\",\n    \"The non-saving in-memory scene-builder seam and clean Unity runner address observed canonical-scene mutation risk from builder tests.\",\n    \"Production GDDRAG freshness and integrity replace the actually stale July Assignment 4 knowledge base.\",\n    \"Scene centralization and meta-identity checks address the earlier duplicate authoritative-scene ambiguity.\",\n    \"Raw-output preservation, bounded retries, finding identities, and deterministic validators address observed reconciliation parsing, max-turn, dangling-edge, attribution, and refinement failures.\",\n    \"Separating task contracts from mutable completion status closes a discovered authority vulnerability, although no bad autonomous dispatch actually occurred.\"\n  ],\n  \"overall_verdict\": \"partially_unsound\",\n  \"overengineering\": [\n    \"Approximately 24.6k lines of pipeline Python precede a single write-capable gameplay execution lane.\",\n    \"A 37-task graph was produced through nine generation workers and up to fifteen parallel auditors, with refiners and selective pass two layered afterward.\",\n    \"The 4,251-line reconciliation prompt suite accumulates specific closure patches, raising maintenance cost and anchoring future reviewers.\",\n    \"AgentRuntime heavily hardens hostile JSON and path cases in a trusted single-user environment while repository writing and approved command execution remain unsupported.\",\n    \"ArchitectureReview provider migration proves transport reuse, not game-development throughput.\",\n    \"WORK_ID_MAP and persisted resource-group membership duplicate relationships derivable from task contracts.\",\n    \"CURRENT_STATE, START_HERE, master context, milestone documents, ADR summaries, and reconciliation current views duplicate present-tense state and have already diverged.\"\n  ],\n  \"state_and_authority_concerns\": [\n    \"Necessary authorities are: human-approved product canon; bounded feature/task contracts; integrated Git state; isolated attempt state; deterministic test/runtime evidence; human experiential approval; and derived readiness/merge state.\",\n    \"The current separation of task definition from completion evidence is sound. Model/provider claims also correctly lack test, merge, or conformance authority.\",\n    \"Dated repository inventory and pipeline architecture should not live in root game-design canon. Put them in generated repository facts and engineering policy.\",\n    \"Reconciliation snapshots are useful audit history, but Tasks, WORK_ID_MAP, RESOURCE_GROUPS, PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS, copied GDD prose, current-state documents, and mutable reconciliation views duplicate responsibility.\",\n    \"Conformance is simultaneously under-sensitive and over-sensitive: manually chosen surfaces may omit causal dependencies, while a whole-GDD hash invalidates unrelated tasks.\",\n    \"Resource-group files duplicate task claims and validate internal agreement rather than whether the referenced Unity resource still exists.\",\n    \"GitHub may become a dashboard, but it must not become a second operational authority without a single explicit owner.\",\n    \"The GDD assigns humans architecture, merging, scene inspection, playtesting, balance, and final integration. The resulting system is assisted development, not high autonomy, until some of those steps are safely narrowed or automated.\"\n  ],\n  \"strong_decisions\": [\n    \"Deterministic tools own graph computation, Git identity, schema checks, test execution facts, and clean-tree checks.\",\n    \"Workers and providers cannot self-declare delivery, conformance, readiness, or authorization.\",\n    \"Runtime and Unity evidence can override a semantic/static pass.\",\n    \"Task definitions are separated from current completion truth.\",\n    \"Branches/worktrees should precede parallelism, and Unity shared-asset locking should be conservative.\",\n    \"RAG is correctly treated as canon retrieval rather than repository truth.\",\n    \"Progressive decomposition and explicit human authority for missing design are sensible principles.\",\n    \"Starting with one worker and bounding retries is appropriate.\",\n    \"The testing policy correctly distinguishes builder capability from committed-artifact conformance.\"\n  ],\n  \"structural_flaws\": [\n    \"Evolving design is framed mainly as snapshot reconciliation and invalidation instead of routine scoped change management.\",\n    \"Verification independence is overstated because workers share canon, candidate framing, prior routing keys, the key registry, provider family, and prescriptive closure prompts.\",\n    \"Unioning every verifier finding reduces false-negative risk but amplifies false-positive cost and destabilizes reconciliation.\",\n    \"The production critical path is missing: no write-capable task execution, orchestrated Unity run, production GER, readiness, dispatch, or merge.\",\n    \"Deterministic checks are strongest at artifact shape and transport boundaries but miss cross-representation referential integrity, as the deleted scene path demonstrates.\",\n    \"The no-op rule can incentivize irrelevant churn. Assignment 6’s final pass added null-target hardening while the substantive framing repair had already occurred elsewhere.\",\n    \"The milestone sequence optimized architecture assurance before feature throughput and has become self-reinforcing.\",\n    \"Provider-neutrality is implemented below an absent product workflow; abstraction arrived before the first production consumer that matters.\"\n  ],\n  \"throughput_assessment\": \"Current near-term leverage is low. The system can retrieve canon, validate graph shape, inspect one camera baseline, and harden model transport, but it cannot autonomously advance gameplay. Dispatch is disabled, workers cannot write or run approved commands, Unity/GER is not integrated, and only one task has production evidence. The machinery may improve assurance after attachment to a delivery lane, but it is not currently credible for turning weeks into days. A thin single-feature lane could plausibly create major leverage quickly; the planned supervisor, decomposer, GitHub, and parallel-worker milestones would not.\",\n  \"verdict_reason\": \"The core philosophy is sound, but the current architecture is partially unsound because its moving-canon and state-synchronization model has already drifted, semantic verification is correlated and prompt-anchored, and most investment lies outside the gameplay-delivery critical path. Preserve deterministic evidence, runtime validation, isolation, bounded retries, and human design authority. Reorganize around one real feature at a time and freeze or remove infrastructure that does not measurably reduce delivery time or escaped defects.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/unity_production_engineer.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/unity_production_engineer.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"alternative_architecture\": {\n    \"description\": \"Reorganize around a thin, single-feature delivery ledger. State: stable requirement IDs/revisions plus Git, small near-frontier contracts, append-only attempt journals, and delivery receipts. Planning: human selects one feature; a planner and Unity scanner validate one contract and its actual paths/GUIDs. Execution: one writable provider in an isolated worktree. Validation: deterministic diff/scope checks, Unity compile, EditMode/PlayMode tests, exact committed-scene inspection, semantic review, and human playtesting. Recovery: preserve branch commits and stage journals, resume from the failed stage, and circuit-break after bounded repairs. Unity integration: serialize only through approved editor/builder operations, retain `.meta` identity, run one host Unity job at a time, and compare canonical asset bytes before/after tests.\",\n    \"needed\": true,\n    \"why_better\": \"This preserves the good safety boundaries but removes global reconciliation, provider parity, artifact generation, GitHub synchronization, and parallel scheduling from the critical path. Requirement-level revisions prevent unrelated GDD edits from invalidating every delivered task, while a task preflight prevents stale Unity paths and GUIDs from entering execution. Most importantly, it produces gameplay evidence before investing further in platform machinery.\"\n  },\n  \"architecture_summary\": \"The core topology is good: bounded workers, Git as integrated truth, deterministic validation, runtime feedback, immutable evidence, and human design/merge authority. The current composition is partially unsound because it lacks an end-to-end writable Unity delivery lane and duplicates mutable project facts across too many stores. An ordinary human-approved scene relocation already left canon, 16 contracts, project requirements, and resource locks pointing at a deleted asset while structural validation still passed.\",\n  \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"No real gameplay task has passed through the current AgentRuntime/TaskExecution architecture. Actual implementation quality, Unity failure rate, repair cost, and end-to-end cycle time remain unmeasured; the camera evidence predates the production execution lane.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.94,\n  \"do_not_build_yet\": [\n    \"Parallel workers, generalized worktree scheduling, or merge queues.\",\n    \"GitHub Issues/Projects synchronization.\",\n    \"Provider fallback or additional provider-comparison/review machinery.\",\n    \"A general Progressive Decomposer and Artifact Authority platform before a real task is blocked by missing design.\",\n    \"Style evaluation or automated artifact generation.\",\n    \"A broad incremental project scanner; add only the Unity/task preflight needed by the next slice.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The checked-out clean tree is exactly the required frozen commit 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Read-only execution of `taskcontrol validate` passed structurally with 37 active contracts, but autonomous dispatch remained disabled. NSC-023 derived `conformant`; NSC-003 derived `not_delivered`. No Unity tests were executed during this review.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Production ExecutionCrew orchestration, Unity execution, task selection, readiness, dispatch, Git workflow, merging, and provider fallback are explicitly unimplemented.\",\n      \"path\": \"Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Both live provider adapters reject repository writing and approved command execution, so AgentRuntime cannot implement a gameplay task.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/AgentRuntime/README.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"TaskExecution only binds a task identity to one invocation; it explicitly owns no selection, Git, Unity, GER, evidence publication, or completion transition.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskExecution/README.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Commit 484e66a moved the populated authoritative scene and its `.meta` identity to `Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity`; commit 86af98f added path/GUID safeguards.\",\n      \"path\": \"Git history at 484e66a and 86af98f\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Sixteen active task contracts still cite the deleted `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity` path.\",\n      \"path\": \"Tasks/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The GDD still declares the deleted nested scene canonical and the populated `Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity` non-canonical, despite the later human-approved relocation.\",\n      \"path\": \"Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Project requirements and the shared scene resource group retain the same obsolete path, so future lock acquisition would not protect the real scene.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml and Pipeline/TaskGraph/RESOURCE_GROUPS.yaml\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The graph validator checks resource-key consistency but not whether `repo-file`, `unity-scene`, repository-evidence, or GUID targets exist.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/work_graph_validate.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The scene-location smoke test correctly protects uniqueness and GUID 92dbd0a3e6c18e245896a66c5120379d, but only scans active C# for obsolete references; it does not cover canon or task metadata.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/Testing/scene_location_smoke_test.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-023 is reported conformant even though its bound contract still contains stale scene repository evidence; its evidence record separately points at the current scene. The evaluator verifies hashes, gates, and listed surfaces, not factual consistency of repository-evidence prose.\",\n      \"path\": \"Tasks/NSC-023.yaml and Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/records/BASE-NSC-023-86af98f41ab5.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Conformance binds the normalized hash of the entire GDD. Any GDD edit stales existing evidence, while only a contract hash/revision change produces `needs_replan`. Conformance surfaces are manually enumerated.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"A passing camera EditMode suite previously invoked the saving production builder and rewrote the canonical tracked scene. The non-saving builder seam and clean runner were created in response.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/Testing/README.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The camera twice passed static evaluation but failed human Unity testing for direction, following, and framing; a later refiner also claimed implementation while making no asset change.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md and Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback000.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Since August 21, reachable history contains roughly 205 commits, only three of which touch Assets; those Asset changes concern test safety and scene centralization rather than a new gameplay feature. The repository has 303 pipeline/task/AI-document files versus 51 tracked Asset files.\",\n      \"path\": \"Git history through 9f2485d\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-003 is classified as `single_agent` despite spanning Input Actions, pointer projection, locomotion, restriction/reset/suspend interfaces, builder wiring, serialized-scene integration, and unresolved input arbitration. Current PlayerMovement still directly polls `Keyboard.current`.\",\n      \"path\": \"Tasks/NSC-003.yaml and Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Unity 6000.1.8f1 is pinned and the Input System is installed, but Build Settings contain no scenes and the required Tilemap/AI Navigation packages are not configured yet.\",\n      \"path\": \"ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt, ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset, and Packages/manifest.json\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"gdd_iteration_concerns\": [\n    \"The architecture currently treats the whole GDD as one versioned unit. An unrelated wording, balance, or room edit will require revalidation of every task whose evidence binds the previous document hash.\",\n    \"A semantic GDD change can require a new contract, different dependencies, implementation rollback, or cancellation. Returning only `needs_revalidation` against the old contract frames design evolution incorrectly.\",\n    \"There are no stable requirement IDs/revisions in canon and no implemented causal mapping from changed requirements to contracts, code/assets, tests, and downstream obligations.\",\n    \"Reconciliation is correctly immutable, but the reviewed graph-delta/apply process for later changes is not implemented. The scene relocation demonstrates that ordinary repository evolution can bypass synchronization.\",\n    \"Repository/process facts should not be copied into root design canon unless maintained transactionally. Canon currently contradicts Git about the authoritative scene.\",\n    \"The future change flow should classify edits as editorial, tuning, additive, changed, or removed; update only affected contracts; supersede contracts when meaning changes; and preserve unaffected delivery evidence.\"\n  ],\n  \"human_intervention_points\": [\n    \"Choose actual product priority; graph order and apparent readiness are not sufficient.\",\n    \"Resolve NSC-003 input arbitration, click-versus-hold semantics, destination replacement/cancellation, arrival/pathing behavior, and restriction ownership before execution.\",\n    \"Approve normative GDD changes and reviewed contract additions, revisions, supersessions, or cancellations.\",\n    \"Inspect scene/prefab references, Inspector assignments, asset imports, and serialized diffs in the Unity Editor.\",\n    \"Run PlayMode checks for game feel, framing, pointer behavior, animation/readability, balance, and Console errors.\",\n    \"Perform Windows build inspection and approve package/build-setting changes.\",\n    \"Review and merge the task branch; automatic merge is not currently justified.\"\n  ],\n  \"hypothetical_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Deep adversarial freezing of provider JSON subtypes and polymorphic containers protects a future hostile/malformed adapter boundary more than an experienced Unity delivery failure.\",\n    \"Cross-provider ArchitectureReview provenance, latest-pointer, and resume support improves review infrastructure but not gameplay throughput.\",\n    \"Provider fallback and cross-provider-equivalent budgeting are future operational concerns.\",\n    \"Parallel worktree claims, GitHub dashboards, merge queues, and continuous scheduling protect a later multi-worker system.\",\n    \"Artifact Authority, generated-design GER, and style evaluation protect future content-generation cases; the current graph contains no initial artifact task and no demonstrated blocking artifact flow.\"\n  ],\n  \"missing_components\": [\n    \"A small approved NSC-003 child contract with resolved input behavior.\",\n    \"A writable, bounded Implementer path using one provider and an isolated branch/worktree.\",\n    \"Task-scoped orchestration from contract to implementation result, deterministic diff, validation, repair, and escalation.\",\n    \"A host Unity execution bridge around the existing clean PowerShell runner.\",\n    \"Deterministic task preflight that validates referenced files, canonical scenes, `.meta` GUIDs, Input Action identities, packages, builder ownership, and Build Settings.\",\n    \"Automatic capture of actual changed paths and scope violations from Git rather than provider claims.\",\n    \"A reviewed evidence publisher that binds actual clean Unity XML, commit/tree, changed surfaces, human approval, and integrated commit.\",\n    \"Requirement-level GDD change-impact analysis and deterministic reviewed contract-delta application.\",\n    \"A derived readiness policy based on current conformance and dependency state; it should remain human-dispatched initially.\"\n  ],\n  \"next_slice\": {\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"Record a human-approved movement micro-brief resolving click/hold input arbitration, destination replacement/cancellation, arrival/pathing, and UI/spell/door click ownership.\",\n      \"Correct the authoritative scene path in canon, affected task/resource metadata, and validation so this drift cannot recur silently.\",\n      \"Supersede or split NSC-003 into a bounded first contract: Input Actions-based pointer projection plus click/hold player movement. Leave restriction/reset/suspend integration as a follow-up if it cannot remain independently testable.\",\n      \"Add only the minimal one-task writable runner needed to invoke one provider in an isolated worktree and capture the real Git diff.\",\n      \"Implement the movement slice in PlayerMovement, Input Actions, the builder, and the authoritative committed scene without regenerating `.meta` identity.\",\n      \"Add isolated component/PlayMode tests plus direct committed-scene conformance checks, run them through the clean Unity host runner, and complete a human feel/input check.\",\n      \"Merge through human review and publish one exact delivery record; use it to prove the first evidence-derived dependency/readiness transition.\"\n    ],\n    \"proof_of_value\": \"One approved gameplay contract travels from planned to implemented, cleanly Unity-tested, human-playtested, merged, evidence-published, and currently conformant at HEAD. Measure elapsed time, model invocations, repair count, human minutes, and escaped defects. That is the first credible evidence that the architecture makes development materially faster.\",\n    \"title\": \"NSC-003A — End-to-End Mouse Movement Delivery Lane\",\n    \"why\": \"This is the smallest slice that exercises the actual value chain and unlocks spells and door interaction. If infrastructure additions were forbidden, the first autonomous failure would be immediate: current providers cannot write or execute commands. With an ordinary human-driven coding agent, the first semantic failure would be NSC-003's unresolved input contract; after implementation, Unity execution and evidence publication would still be manual. This slice exposes and closes all three gaps without building the later platform.\"\n  },\n  \"observed_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Runtime Unity feedback feeding GER solved camera direction, following, and framing defects missed by static semantic review.\",\n    \"The no-op progress guard prevented a refiner's unchanged `implemented` claim from being accepted and charged it against the repair budget.\",\n    \"The non-saving in-memory builder seam separated builder capability from canonical serialized state and stopped tests from saving the production scene.\",\n    \"Direct committed-scene tests, byte comparison, and the clean runner address the experienced destructive passing-test incident.\",\n    \"Moving the scene with its `.meta`, asserting one authoritative scene, and checking the GUID address the duplicate-scene/asset-identity incident.\",\n    \"Immutable reconciliation runs, recovery scripts, bounded retries, and deterministic finding identities address actual max-turn, timeout, interrupted-run, and ordering failures visible in history.\",\n    \"Schema-v2 contracts and committed conformance evidence prevent a mutable task status or worker claim from becoming completion truth, although autonomous dispatch had not yet experienced that failure in production.\",\n    \"Production GDDRAG fixes the demonstrated staleness of the older Assignment 4 index, while correctly not claiming top-k coverage completeness.\"\n  ],\n  \"overall_verdict\": \"partially_unsound\",\n  \"overengineering\": [\n    \"The project implemented two live provider adapters and migrated an eight-role architecture-review consumer before enabling one provider to write a gameplay change.\",\n    \"The accepted staged plan requires both adapters and full ExecutionCrew roles before the first real provider-interchangeable task proof; that ordering optimizes abstraction confidence ahead of product throughput.\",\n    \"Global multi-model reconciliation, verification, streaming refinement, recovery, migrations, and immutable histories consumed substantial effort for a 37-contract bootstrap. They should not become the normal response to iterative GDD edits.\",\n    \"There are multiple overlapping architecture/status documents with already-stale milestone claims. Operational status should be generated from code where possible.\",\n    \"The repository's recent commit/file ratio shows infrastructure becoming a second product: extensive pipeline churn has not produced a post-camera gameplay feature.\",\n    \"Provider trust-boundary hardening is technically strong, but much of its depth has lower current expected value than a writable task lane, Unity preflight, and one real gameplay delivery.\"\n  ],\n  \"state_and_authority_concerns\": [\n    \"Necessary boundaries: approved GDD/feature decisions define intent; task contracts define bounded work and gates; Git defines integrated code/assets; attempt journals define transient execution; evidence receipts plus current Git derive conformance; humans own ambiguous design, visual/feel approval, and merge authority.\",\n    \"Necessary separation: generic AgentRuntime must not own NSC task identity, and TaskExecution audit artifacts must not become completion evidence. The ADR-039 direction is correct.\",\n    \"Duplicated responsibility: authoritative scene paths and other repository facts appear in GDD prose, task repository evidence, exclusive-resource keys, project requirements, RAG chunks, current-state documents, and conformance surfaces.\",\n    \"The living graph is called operational state, but schema v2 intentionally contains no operational status and readiness is disabled. It is currently a contract/dependency catalog, not an operational work graph.\",\n    \"Manual conformance-surface selection can under-approximate Unity dependencies. A package, Input Action, prefab, ProjectSetting, referenced ScriptableObject, or builder dependency omitted from the receipt can change without invalidating conformance.\",\n    \"Reconciliation history should remain audit history, but it should not be a prerequisite authority layer for routine local changes. A requirement/task delta plus human approval is enough.\",\n    \"GitHub should remain a projection/dashboard if introduced; it must not duplicate contract, evidence, or conformance authority.\"\n  ],\n  \"strong_decisions\": [\n    \"One bounded worker and human merge authority before parallelism are appropriate for Unity's shared serialized assets.\",\n    \"Workers and provider adapters produce claims, never test, merge, readiness, or completion truth.\",\n    \"Task contracts are separated from mutable completion status, and committed Git objects—not a dirty working copy—drive conformance evaluation.\",\n    \"Exact commit/tree, artifact blob, task revision, canon hash, and human approval binding make evidence auditable.\",\n    \"Unity tests must start and finish with a clean tree, parse XML, preserve logs, and report exact Unity version and limitations.\",\n    \"Builder output and committed serialized artifact conformance are treated as separate claims.\",\n    \"Scenes and prefabs move with `.meta` files; duplicate authoritative scenes are prohibited.\",\n    \"Runtime and visual evidence can override a static evaluator pass and return structured feedback to bounded repair.\",\n    \"Missing design is escalated rather than silently invented.\",\n    \"Parallelism, automatic merge, and autonomous dispatch remain disabled until a one-ticket lane is trustworthy.\"\n  ],\n  \"structural_flaws\": [\n    \"There is no end-to-end state machine connecting approved plan → writable execution → Git-observed changes → Unity validation → repair → human review → merge → delivery evidence → dependency readiness.\",\n    \"Moving canon is modeled as whole-document invalidation, not requirement-level versioning and impact. This will generate broad revalidation work while still failing to revise contracts whose meaning changed.\",\n    \"Unity asset authority is duplicated and already contradictory after one routine human-approved relocation. Structural validation passes because it verifies key consistency rather than target existence and GUID identity.\",\n    \"The current graph can label an item `single_agent` without resolving gameplay choices or proving its Unity integration surface is bounded; NSC-003 is the concrete counterexample.\",\n    \"A conformance record can be current while descriptive repository evidence inside its bound contract is factually stale, because only hashes, declared gates, and manually selected surfaces are evaluated.\",\n    \"The production runtime deliberately cannot write or execute approved commands, and the Unity runner remains an external manual operation. Therefore the current architecture is an audit/review platform, not an autonomous development pipeline.\",\n    \"The milestone ordering puts compact context, decomposition, provider parity, and generalized crew infrastructure ahead of the shortest working gameplay loop, despite the stated real-slice rule.\",\n    \"Worktree isolation alone does not solve Unity production integration. Each candidate still needs a viable Unity project/library strategy, serialized asset inspection, exclusive host execution, and post-rebase validation.\"\n  ],\n  \"throughput_assessment\": \"Major near-term leverage is not yet demonstrated. The architecture has materially improved safety, repeatability, and auditability, especially around Unity tests and camera validation. But at the frozen commit, zero tasks are dispatchable, providers cannot edit, NSC-003 is not safely specified, Unity validation is external, and evidence publication is manual. Recent history is overwhelmingly pipeline work with no new gameplay feature. The system is therefore slower than direct expert-assisted development today; it becomes plausibly high-leverage only after the proposed vertical slice proves a repeatable one-ticket cycle.\",\n  \"verdict_reason\": \"The foundational production instincts are strong, so this is not a fundamentally wrong approach. It is partially unsound because moving-design synchronization, Unity asset authority, and the execution-to-evidence path are either incorrectly framed or absent. The existing stale-scene contradiction is direct evidence that the current stores do not stay coherent, and the lack of a writable Unity delivery lane means the architecture cannot yet achieve its stated days-instead-of-weeks goal.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/workflow_systems_architect.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"alternative_architecture\": {\n    \"description\": \"Replace the milestone-platform plan with a thin vertical-slice delivery coordinator. State: stable requirement IDs/revisions in the GDD, small Git-versioned task contracts, one local SQLite/event journal for transient execution, Git commits as implementation truth, immutable external validation manifests, and GitHub as a rebuildable projection only. Planning: keep a rolling three-to-five-item frontier chosen by the human; decompose only the selected slice. Execution: one write-capable agent in one branch/worktree. Validation: deterministic Git checks, Windows Unity compile/EditMode/PlayMode runs, one semantic diff review, then human experiential review. Recovery: explicit compare-and-set transitions with idempotency keys—planned, claimed, workspace_ready, implemented, validating, needs_human, merge_ready, merged, verified—and reconciliation against actual Git refs/PR state after restart. GDD changes: diff stable requirement revisions and invalidate only referencing tasks/evidence plus affected dependents. Unity integration: one Windows host adapter owns the Editor lock, exact Unity version, result XML/log collection, repository-mutation checks, and scene/prefab inspection handoff.\",\n    \"needed\": true,\n    \"why_better\": \"It preserves the architecture’s valuable evidence boundaries while eliminating global canon invalidation, duplicated operational truth, speculative platform layers, and the missing crash-recovery semantics. It optimizes for repeated delivery of playable slices rather than exhaustive roadmap correctness.\"\n  },\n  \"architecture_summary\": \"The foundational principles are mostly sound: deterministic state outside model context, bounded workers, Git-based integration, immutable evidence, one worker initially, and Unity/runtime evidence that can override model confidence. The assembled system is nevertheless partially unsound as an autonomous development architecture. Its canonical scene identity is already split across GDD, task contracts, resource locks, tests, and current-state documentation; any GDD edit globally invalidates conformance; readiness and dispatch are disabled; and no durable execution/Git/Unity lifecycle joins planning to delivery. It is currently an audit platform, not a game-development accelerator.\",\n  \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"No current end-to-end gameplay ticket has traversed selection, implementation, Unity validation, repair, merge, and post-merge evidence, so actual time savings and human orchestration cost remain unmeasured. Availability and reliability of the Windows Unity host is the largest practical unknown.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.94,\n  \"do_not_build_yet\": [\n    \"GitHub Projects dependency/status mirroring or bidirectional synchronization\",\n    \"Parallel workers, leases, merge queues, or generalized resource scheduling\",\n    \"A bundled Progressive Decomposer and Artifact Authority platform without a real blocking design artifact\",\n    \"More reconciliation auditor roles, streaming repair machinery, or full-game graph verification\",\n    \"Provider fallback or another provider transport layer\",\n    \"Incremental scanner/cache infrastructure beyond targeted rg/Git/Unity queries\",\n    \"Automatic merge or automatic evidence approval\",\n    \"Assignment 7/style integration until real authorized player-facing content needs it\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The frozen commit and working tree were confirmed as 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e and clean before and after diagnostic commands.\",\n      \"path\": \".git\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The GDD declares the deleted Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity authoritative and calls Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity a non-canonical stub.\",\n      \"path\": \"Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The actual builder, committed-scene tests, scene-location smoke test, conformance evidence, and CURRENT_STATE all treat Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity as authoritative.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Thirteen task resource claims reference the deleted old scene. A read-only scan found 36 concrete resource-path claims, 13 missing. NSC-037 explicitly requires registering the nonexistent scene and forbids using the actual authoritative one.\",\n      \"path\": \"Tasks/NSC-037.yaml\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Despite that split brain, taskcontrol validate and the schema-v2 quality audit both report PASS; the independent scene-location smoke test also reports PASS. Each validates a locally consistent island but not cross-source authority.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/work_graph_validate.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The graph validator checks resource-key membership consistency but not whether concrete repo-file/unity-scene paths exist.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/work_graph_validate.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Conformance compares every record with the normalized hash of the entire GDD; any canon edit marks otherwise unchanged evidence stale.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Current history contains 13 GDD-changing commits with 466 added and 117 deleted lines, making moving-canon handling an experienced condition rather than a remote possibility.\",\n      \"path\": \"Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"taskcontrol validate passes, but taskcontrol ready reports readiness unavailable and zero authorized tasks. authorize always denies.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"TaskExecution accepts caller-supplied task identity/hash, writes a wrapper artifact, and delegates one invocation. It does not resolve the contract from committed HEAD or implement task lifecycle, retries, Git, validation, or recovery.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskExecution/task_runner.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Reconciliation emits taskcontrol_diff_required when Tasks exist, but the deterministic snapshot-to-contract diff/review/apply mechanism it calls for is not implemented.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/Reconciliation/reconciliation_agent.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Reconciliation current views and latest pointers are convenience copies written across multiple files without one atomic publication transaction; a crash can expose a mixed view, although immutable run directories limit historical damage.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/Reconciliation/output_layout.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Assignment 6 records two static-evaluator passes followed by Unity runtime failures and one no-op refinement before human-guided repair succeeded.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The current Unity gameplay remains a small door/mana/camera prototype; PlayerMovement still directly polls WASD. After Assignment 6, Assets history primarily contains camera test safety and scene-centralization changes rather than new player-facing systems.\",\n      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"A scale inspection counted approximately 31,384 non-output/non-JSON text lines under Pipeline, 3,283 task-contract lines, and 4,445 AI-pipeline documentation lines, versus about 1,595 C# lines under Assets/NoSafeCircle. This is a scale signal, not a productivity metric.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Production GDDRAG status and validation pass with 41 hash-current chunks, but integrity against the GDD cannot detect that the GDD’s canonical-scene claim is stale against Git.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/GDDRAG/integrity.py\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-023 currently derives as conformant from committed evidence. The human approval is represented only by a nonempty name and prose inside a repository JSON record, not an independently authenticated approval receipt.\",\n      \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/records/BASE-NSC-023-86af98f41ab5.json\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"gdd_iteration_concerns\": [\n    \"A one-byte unrelated GDD change currently makes every delivered task that binds the document hash need revalidation.\",\n    \"Task contracts cite prose sections rather than stable requirement IDs/revisions, so scoped causal impact cannot be calculated deterministically.\",\n    \"The repository already demonstrates failure to propagate an engineering change—the canonical scene move—into the GDD, 22 task references, and resource groups.\",\n    \"Reconciliation is still framed primarily as global snapshot correctness. Routine iteration needs requirement-level change sets: added, modified, removed, clarified, and implementation-impact classification.\",\n    \"Existing planned work needs explicit outcomes after canon change: unaffected, needs_contract_revision, cancelled, superseded, implementation_migration_required, or revalidation_only.\",\n    \"Approved subordinate design artifacts need their own versioned requirement identities and dependency edges; merely hashing files recreates global invalidation at a smaller scale.\"\n  ],\n  \"human_intervention_points\": [\n    \"Choose the next player-visible slice and resolve ambiguous input, UX, architecture, and balance decisions.\",\n    \"Approve requirement/contract revisions and migration impact after material GDD changes.\",\n    \"Inspect scene, prefab, Input Actions, package, Build Settings, and serialized-reference changes.\",\n    \"Run or supervise experiential Play Mode checks for feel, framing, readability, timing, and visuals.\",\n    \"Review and merge PRs until automated delivery has a substantial successful track record.\",\n    \"Resolve repeated repairs, Unity host failures, merge conflicts, scope expansion, and budget exhaustion.\",\n    \"Approve generated design/art before it becomes canon and judge final game quality.\"\n  ],\n  \"hypothetical_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Exclusive-resource groups, leases, and future parallel-worker scheduling mostly protect against concurrency that is not yet enabled.\",\n    \"GitHub Issues/Projects mirroring, Draft-PR automation, and merge queues address future operational scale, not a current single-worker bottleneck.\",\n    \"Progressive decomposition and Artifact Authority protect against future missing-design invention; no production artifact flow currently demonstrates the need for the whole platform.\",\n    \"Extensive provider-polymorphism, malformed-container, Windows-path-alias, and fallback boundaries are valuable hardening but mostly protect future write-capable consumers; current providers are read-only for production work.\",\n    \"Full-game reconciliation with fifteen auditors and field-level streaming repair protects semantic bootstrap completeness, but ordinary feature delivery does not require repeating it.\",\n    \"Project-state caches and generic scanners target future token/latency savings; the current GDD and codebase remain small enough for bounded direct retrieval.\"\n  ],\n  \"missing_components\": [\n    \"One authoritative canonical-scene/project-state registry and a migration that updates GDD, tasks, locks, and build requirements together\",\n    \"Stable requirement IDs and scoped GDD impact/revision semantics\",\n    \"A legal operational transition table with compare-and-set rules, idempotency keys, retry ownership, and terminal states\",\n    \"A durable single-ticket attempt journal joining task, branch, commits, provider runs, validation, human decisions, PR, merge, and evidence\",\n    \"A contract resolver that verifies task revision/hash against committed HEAD rather than accepting caller assertions\",\n    \"A write-capable bounded gameplay execution path\",\n    \"A Windows Unity host adapter with Editor locking, exact-version checks, XML/log collection, timeout handling, and clean-tree verification\",\n    \"Deterministic reconciliation-to-task diff/review/apply tooling, if global reconciliation is retained\",\n    \"Git/GitHub projection reconciliation and crash-window handling\",\n    \"Measured delivery telemetry: elapsed time, model cost, human minutes, retries, Unity import/test time, and escaped defects\"\n  ],\n  \"next_slice\": {\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"First correct the canonical-scene split brain in one reviewed migration: GDD, affected task contracts, resource groups, NSC-037, and validation checks.\",\n      \"Deliver NSC-005 Player Mana reset and denied-cast feedback on one ordinary branch using a direct bounded coding worker; do not build a supervisor first.\",\n      \"Extend the existing PlayMode tests for reset, regeneration-delay reset, denied-spend signal, and readable low-mana feedback; run the clean Windows Unity wrapper and preserve XML/logs outside the repository.\",\n      \"Perform human Play Mode validation, merge, then create one post-merge delivery evidence manifest and record elapsed time, retries, cost, and human minutes.\",\n      \"Write the observed lifecycle as a tiny append-only run journal; implement coordinator code only for a failure that actually occurred during this slice.\"\n    ],\n    \"proof_of_value\": \"Within one working day, NSC-005 is merged and demonstrably playable; clean Unity evidence is reproducible at the merged commit; the workflow can resume after one deliberately interrupted model invocation without duplicate commits or lost state; no more than two repair calls are needed; and human orchestration is measured rather than estimated.\",\n    \"title\": \"Repair authority drift, then ship NSC-005 through a no-new-platform delivery lane\",\n    \"why\": \"NSC-005 extends existing runtime code and PlayMode tests without Input Actions, package changes, or scene-builder ownership. It exercises implementation, feedback, Unity validation, repair, merge, and evidence while avoiding NSC-003’s unresolved control semantics and the stale scene locks. It is the fastest honest test of whether the pipeline saves development time.\"\n  },\n  \"observed_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Runtime evidence re-entering GER directly addresses the two camera implementations that passed static review but failed in Unity.\",\n    \"The no-op progress guard and bounded circuit breaker address the recorded Refiner claim of implementation without an Assets change.\",\n    \"The clean Unity runner and non-saving test seams address prior test-process waiting, repository-mutation, and canonical-scene safety problems reflected in repository history.\",\n    \"Immutable reconciliation runs, resume utilities, deterministic finding IDs, and conflict handling address actual max-turn, recovery, identity, and merge defects encountered while bootstrapping reconciliation.\",\n    \"The production GDDRAG integrity boundary addresses the historical Assignment 4 index becoming stale after substantial GDD revision.\",\n    \"Provider-scoped immutable ArchitectureReview runs and AgentRuntime normalization solve real audit-workflow provenance, timeout, and provider-output transport needs, though not gameplay delivery.\"\n  ],\n  \"overall_verdict\": \"partially_unsound\",\n  \"overengineering\": [\n    \"The project built an exhaustive 37-contract graph, multi-model reconciliation, recovery layers, streaming field repair, provider abstraction, and architecture-review migration before one current gameplay ticket could be dispatched.\",\n    \"Fifteen reconciliation auditors produced 56 first-pass findings and required bespoke refinement/conflict tooling. That improved bootstrap quality but is disproportionate for a rolling single-developer gameplay backlog.\",\n    \"Task resource claims are duplicated again in RESOURCE_GROUPS.yaml; exact agreement is validated, but existence and current ownership are not.\",\n    \"TaskExecution duplicates the full invocation in task_request.json while AgentRuntime publishes request.json separately, without a transaction joining their completion.\",\n    \"Provider trust-boundary hardening is much deeper than the missing vertical delivery path: repository writing, Git, Unity, task selection, and recovery remain absent.\",\n    \"Current/LATEST reconciliation views add several mutable projections whose authority must continually be explained.\",\n    \"The planned Milestone 2 bundles RAG, scanner, context packs, decomposition, artifact authority, generation, GER, and promotion despite CURRENT_STATE correctly warning against building most of it speculatively.\"\n  ],\n  \"state_and_authority_concerns\": [\n    \"Necessary separation: GDD/design authority, task definition, integrated Git state, validation evidence, transient execution, and human approval should remain distinct.\",\n    \"The GDD currently contains mutable repository-location and pipeline-operation facts. Those belong in an engineering/project-state registry referenced by design requirements, not duplicated as game-design canon.\",\n    \"Tasks contain historical repository evidence and stale concrete paths while current Git and conformance evidence say something else; bootstrap observations are being mistaken for durable integration contracts.\",\n    \"Task resource ownership is stored in task files and RESOURCE_GROUPS.yaml. One should be derived from the other.\",\n    \"Future GitHub state must be a projection of one operational ledger. If both are writable authorities, claim, retry, close, and merge races become ambiguous.\",\n    \"Conformance surfaces are manually enumerated. Omitted dependencies can change without invalidating evidence, producing false conformance; unrelated listed files can produce needless revalidation.\",\n    \"A plain approved_by string inside a repository record is an approval assertion, not authenticated human authority unless protected review/merge policy supplies that authority externally.\",\n    \"The transition from merged code to a later committed evidence record has no owned crash-recovery receipt. A merge can succeed while the task remains not_delivered indefinitely.\",\n    \"AgentRuntime run directories are immutable but incomplete directories after interruption are not resumable under the same run ID; a higher coordinator must classify and reconcile these orphans.\",\n    \"CURRENT_STATE and START_HERE contain mutually stale milestone directions and readiness claims, weakening the repository-as-truth principle.\"\n  ],\n  \"strong_decisions\": [\n    \"Models do not own durable state or declare their own success.\",\n    \"Integrated Git state is distinguished from model claims and builder capability.\",\n    \"Task definition is separated from mutable operational/completion state.\",\n    \"Deterministic validation precedes expensive semantic review where practical.\",\n    \"Unity/runtime evidence can override a static evaluator pass.\",\n    \"Normal tests must not mutate canonical Unity assets, and clean-tree state is part of validation.\",\n    \"One worker precedes parallelism; branches/worktrees protect main.\",\n    \"Reconciliation history is immutable and cannot directly rewrite task contracts.\",\n    \"Missing design is escalated rather than silently invented.\",\n    \"Retries and repair loops are bounded, and no-op repair consumes budget.\",\n    \"Generic provider invocation is separated from No Safe Circle task identity.\"\n  ],\n  \"structural_flaws\": [\n    \"Canonical-scene authority is already contradictory across root canon, tasks, resource locks, actual assets, tests, and evidence. The validators all pass because no cross-boundary invariant covers it.\",\n    \"Whole-document canon hashing makes synchronization with iterative design equivalent to global revalidation.\",\n    \"There is no operational state machine connecting active contract, readiness, claim, attempt, validation, human review, merge, delivery evidence, dependency satisfaction, retry, cancellation, or recovery.\",\n    \"The architecture specifies Git branches, worktrees, GitHub Issues/PRs, and leases without defining idempotent external side effects, compare-and-set ownership, duplicate-event handling, or restart reconciliation.\",\n    \"Current schema 2.0 removed unsafe status but did not replace it with dependency-satisfaction semantics; the graph therefore cannot answer the question it was built to answer.\",\n    \"The reconciliation-to-task delta boundary is a documented call to an unimplemented tool.\",\n    \"TaskExecution does not prove that its supplied contract identity is current or approved.\",\n    \"Manual conformance-surface selection cannot prove completeness of the change-impact boundary.\",\n    \"Mutable convenience views and pointers are not atomically published as one generation, allowing mixed current views after interruption.\",\n    \"Documentation routing has accumulated incompatible current plans, so agents cannot safely infer the active architecture from START_HERE and milestone files alone.\"\n  ],\n  \"throughput_assessment\": \"Major near-term leverage is not currently plausible. The frozen system authorizes zero tasks and lacks repository-write, Git, Unity-host, merge, and recovery orchestration. Since the historical GER camera delivery, infrastructure expanded far more than playable code, and the first post-bootstrap engineering change already desynchronized canon and task state. The bounded-agent/GER approach can plausibly speed individual coding and repair, as the camera experiment suggests, but “days instead of weeks” requires repeated measured deliveries. Until two or three real slices complete through the same thin lane, the architecture is producing safety documentation and audit leverage—not development throughput.\",\n  \"verdict_reason\": \"Do not discard the core principles, but reject the current milestone sequence and state model. The architecture is not fundamentally wrong in choosing deterministic ownership and bounded workers; it is partially unsound because its moving-canon semantics, canonical-resource authority, lifecycle transitions, and recovery boundaries fail under conditions the repository has already experienced. Reorganize around a small requirement-versioned delivery ledger and a real Unity vertical slice before adding more infrastructure.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'jq .result Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/codex/runs/20260823T102213Z-35a76864/reviews/yagni_complexity_critic.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"alternative_architecture\": {\n    \"description\": \"Replace the subsystem roadmap with a thin gameplay-delivery lane. State consists of: the human-approved GDD/decision records, small task contracts, integrated main, one append-only attempt/receipt per delivery, and explicit human approvals. Planning is human-prioritized and just-in-time; a single coding worker plans and implements one bounded slice in an isolated worktree. Validation combines scope/diff checks, the existing clean Unity runner, task-specific Edit/Play Mode tests, one fresh semantic review, and a human game-feel/visual gate. Failures return one feedback bundle through at most two repairs. Recovery uses the worktree, checkpoint commits, and a small attempt manifest. After human merge, a receipt binds the integrated commit, relevant canon excerpts, contract revision, test XML, and approval. Readiness derives from merged receipts. GDD edits trigger scoped diff review of referenced sections, not global reconciliation or global invalidation.\",\n    \"needed\": true,\n    \"why_better\": \"It covers state, planning, execution, validation, recovery, and Unity integration while reusing the mechanisms that addressed observed failures. It removes multiple speculative authority and orchestration layers, creates player-visible evidence quickly, and treats design evolution as routine synchronization rather than repeated bootstrap verification.\"\n  },\n  \"architecture_summary\": \"The architectural kernel is sound: durable state, bounded workers, deterministic checks, Git isolation, Unity/runtime evidence, human authority, and bounded repair are appropriate. The current composition is partially unsound and substantially overbuilt. It has accumulated planning, reconciliation, evidence, provider, and audit machinery without an executable gameplay-delivery path. Worse, its claimed authorities already disagree: canon and task resources name a deleted scene, current code uses the replacement, structural validation still passes, and primary status documents disagree about readiness and implementation stage.\",\n  \"biggest_uncertainty\": \"Actual wall-clock leverage remains unmeasured. No real gameplay task has traversed planning, model implementation, Unity validation, repair, merge, and derived completion using the production architecture; the only production conformance record is a retrospective camera baseline.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.95,\n  \"do_not_build_yet\": [\n    \"Continuous supervisor or automatic backlog replenishment.\",\n    \"Parallel workers, claims/leases, resource scheduling, merge queues, or automatic merge.\",\n    \"GitHub Issues/Projects synchronization beyond optional manual links.\",\n    \"Progressive Decomposer and Artifact Authority platform until a real task is blocked by missing design; the initial graph has zero artifact tasks.\",\n    \"More reconciliation verification/refinement machinery for routine changes.\",\n    \"Provider fallback, provider-comparison automation, or additional provider abstractions.\",\n    \"Roslyn/general-purpose project scanning or incremental caches before targeted shell/repository inspection proves insufficient.\",\n    \"More architecture-review orchestration.\",\n    \"Additional immutable evidence layers beyond one concise delivery receipt and underlying test artifacts.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The frozen commit validates 37 structurally well-formed contracts, but readiness is explicitly unavailable and every autonomous dispatch is denied.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py:180\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The canonical GDD says the nested DoorPrototype scene is authoritative and the top-level scene is a stub, but that nested scene was deleted and the production builder now saves the populated authoritative scene at Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:233\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The production builder directly establishes the new authoritative scene path.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:16\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Thirteen task resource claims, PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS, and RESOURCE_GROUPS still lock the deleted scene path. The build-registration task explicitly rejects the scene that is now authoritative. Nevertheless, taskcontrol validate reports PASS.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/RESOURCE_GROUPS.yaml:108\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"NSC-037's current contract describes Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity as a non-canonical stub, contradicting the integrated tree.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Tasks/NSC-037.yaml:20\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Conformance binds every delivery to the hash of the entire GDD. Any unrelated GDD edit makes all evidenced tasks stale, while a hash match cannot detect that the GDD itself contains obsolete repository claims.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:164\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Conformance evaluates the current HEAD and ancestry but does not verify an integration ref such as main. At the frozen commit, HEAD is provider-adapters while main is at a2b7a78, so 'integrated' cannot be derived from the implemented check alone.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py:134\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"START_HERE claims taskcontrol ready works and identifies seven executable tasks; CURRENT_STATE and the implementation say readiness is unavailable. CURRENT_STATE also says Codex migration is implemented near its beginning but later calls Codex unimplemented and migration the next goal.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Docs/AI-Pipeline/START_HERE.md:44\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Since the Assignment 6 camera commit, 126 commits touched Pipeline/Docs/Tasks but only two touched Assets, both scene/test hardening rather than a new gameplay feature. The diff adds about 115,877 lines to Pipeline/Docs/Tasks.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/.git\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Current production machinery is about 9,466 Python lines across TaskGraph, AgentRuntime, TaskExecution, and GDDRAG; Reconciliation adds about 15,195 lines, before generated outputs. Task contracts add about 3,283 lines for a prototype with roughly 11 gameplay scripts.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The actual movement implementation still polls Keyboard.current for WASD and has none of the mouse movement, shared pointer projection, restriction, reset, or suspension behavior in NSC-003.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs:22\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The scene builder still displays 'Hold E to Open', while current canon requires click-to-approach and automatic opening on arrival. This shows substantial gameplay drift remains despite extensive planning machinery.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:250\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Assignment 6 records two cases where static evaluation passed an unusable camera and one case where a refiner claimed success without changing Assets. Human Unity testing and a no-progress guard caught them.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:77\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"The production RAG is current and integrity-checked, but explicitly does not implement task context, scanning, decomposition, artifact authority, supervision, GER integration, or gameplay work.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/GDDRAG/README.md:196\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"AgentRuntime currently cannot grant repository writing or approved command execution, so TaskExecution cannot implement or validate gameplay even if a task is manually selected.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md:46\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"observation\": \"Only NSC-023 has committed production conformance evidence; NSC-003 reports not_delivered and no real production revalidation record exists.\",\n      \"path\": \"/workspace/Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-023/records/BASE-NSC-023-86af98f41ab5.json:1\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"gdd_iteration_concerns\": [\n    \"The GDD mixes durable game design with volatile repository facts such as canonical scene paths. Those facts changed without the GDD changing, creating two alleged authorities that now contradict each other.\",\n    \"Whole-GDD hashing is safe but too blunt: an unrelated balance, prose, or distant-room edit invalidates every completed task, while an unchanged hash provides no semantic guarantee that repository facts embedded in canon remain true.\",\n    \"Task contracts copy large requirement passages from the GDD. This creates another synchronization surface instead of stable references to governing sections or requirement identities.\",\n    \"For planned work, a relevant GDD change should revise or supersede the task. For active work, it should pause the attempt and require a scoped rebase decision. For delivered work, it should create focused revalidation/remediation only when governing requirements or implementation surfaces changed.\",\n    \"Routine GDD changes need a reviewed impact diff over referenced headings/excerpts. Full reconciliation should be reserved for broad redesign, an exhausted backlog, milestone audits, or suspected systemic drift.\",\n    \"The existing architecture documents acknowledge that scoped impact analysis is eventual, but moving-target synchronization is a current need: the repository already demonstrates canon/task/scene drift.\"\n  ],\n  \"human_intervention_points\": [\n    \"Choose the next player-valued priority; the graph cannot infer product priority.\",\n    \"Resolve NSC-003 input ownership, UI/door/spell click arbitration, click-versus-hold semantics, cancellation, arrival behavior, pathing expectations, and movement-restriction ownership.\",\n    \"Approve GDD changes and classify ambiguous downstream impact.\",\n    \"Judge movement feel, camera framing, readability, visuals, animation, audio, and interaction quality in Play Mode.\",\n    \"Handle Unity serialization/editor work where safe deterministic builders or tests cannot establish the intended result.\",\n    \"Approve architecture-changing prerequisites, shared-resource changes, scope expansion, and generated design/content.\",\n    \"Review and merge early delivery attempts; automatic merge is not justified.\",\n    \"Provide or maintain the Unity host/toolchain and inspect failures that batch execution cannot resolve.\"\n  ],\n  \"hypothetical_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Claims, leases, resource scheduling, and conservative parallelism protect against future multi-worker conflicts; no production multi-worker delivery exists.\",\n    \"GitHub dashboard mirroring, Draft-PR lifecycle automation, merge queues, and continuous scheduling solve future operational scale rather than a present delivery bottleneck.\",\n    \"Artifact Authority, artifact promotion, and artifact GER protect against generated-design misuse, but the initial graph has zero artifact tasks and no observed blocking artifact flow.\",\n    \"Provider fallback and cross-provider equivalence policies solve future availability/portability concerns, not Unity gameplay throughput.\",\n    \"The breadth of immutable provider artifacts, schema attack hardening, historical-directory protection, and anti-tamper evidence checks exceeds failures observed in this trusted single-user project.\",\n    \"A full multi-model reconciliation verification/refinement crew was defensible for the one-time bootstrap, but repeated use for ordinary GDD evolution would address hypothetical semantic corruption at disproportionate cost.\",\n    \"General project scanners, Roslyn analysis, incremental caches, and automatic causal impact models remain speculative until direct repository inspection becomes a measured bottleneck.\"\n  ],\n  \"missing_components\": [\n    \"A write-capable gameplay worker boundary; current providers are read/search only.\",\n    \"A minimal one-task coordinator that creates/resumes an isolated worktree and records checkpoints.\",\n    \"A truthful readiness rule deriving dependency satisfaction from integrated delivery receipts.\",\n    \"A host-side Unity compile/EditMode/PlayMode validation handoff tied to the task attempt.\",\n    \"A merge-time receipt path that connects task, relevant canon, integrated commit, tests, and human approval.\",\n    \"Scoped GDD/task impact handling and task supersession for moving canon.\",\n    \"Task-specific tests and scene/action integration for the first real gameplay slice.\",\n    \"Throughput telemetry: elapsed time, model time, human minutes, retries, Unity cycles, and failure causes.\"\n  ],\n  \"next_slice\": {\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"Human-approved micro-contract resolving NSC-003 input arbitration, cancellation, arrival/pathing, and restriction semantics; supersede the current overconfident single-agent contract rather than dispatching it unchanged.\",\n      \"Use one manually created writable worktree and one coding worker; add no new supervisor, graph, reconciliation, or provider framework.\",\n      \"Implement Input Actions-backed pointer projection plus click-to-destination/held-pointer movement, restriction, suspension, and reset in PlayerMovement, with canonical scene/builder wiring.\",\n      \"Add focused pure/component and Play Mode tests mapped to the micro-contract, then run the existing clean Unity validation path and preserve XML outside the repository during execution.\",\n      \"Perform one human Play Mode movement-feel and input-conflict check.\",\n      \"Merge manually and publish one concise delivery receipt using existing evidence concepts.\",\n      \"Record elapsed time, agent time, human minutes, retries, context size, Unity cycles, and every manual intervention.\"\n    ],\n    \"proof_of_value\": \"From a clean base, complete and merge the playable slice within one working day with no pipeline-framework code, no canonical-asset mutation during tests, passing compile/EditMode/PlayMode evidence, and no more than about 60 minutes of human orchestration. Restart the worker once from a checkpoint to prove recovery. If this cannot be done, the observed blocker—not the roadmap—defines the next infrastructure slice.\",\n    \"title\": \"Gameplay-first mouse movement delivery experiment\",\n    \"why\": \"It attacks a visible current GDD/repository gap, exercises Input Actions, shared targeting, scene integration, Unity validation, repair, recovery, and truthful completion, and will reveal whether the existing machinery provides leverage or merely overhead.\"\n  },\n  \"observed_failure_solutions\": [\n    \"Repository-persisted state addresses real cross-session and stale-assignment problems better than conversation memory.\",\n    \"The current GDD RAG rejects the genuinely stale Assignment 4 knowledge base and binds retrieval to current canon.\",\n    \"Runtime feedback re-entering GER solved two camera implementations that passed static review but failed in Unity.\",\n    \"The no-op progress guard solved an observed refiner that claimed implementation without changing Assets.\",\n    \"Bounded retries/circuit breaking addressed the demonstrated possibility of repeated failed camera refinements.\",\n    \"The non-saving builder seam, committed-scene conformance tests, clean Unity runner, and scene centralization address observed test-mutation and duplicate-scene-authority risks.\",\n    \"Removing mutable completion from task contracts and deriving conformance from committed evidence addresses a real unsafe authority design, although it has not yet been connected back to readiness.\",\n    \"Deterministic graph validation usefully catches malformed identity, dependency cycles, and structural corruption, though it does not catch semantic/path drift.\"\n  ],\n  \"overall_verdict\": \"partially_unsound\",\n  \"overengineering\": [\n    \"Approximately 28,000 lines of current pipeline and reconciliation Python precede the first production gameplay delivery loop.\",\n    \"The one-time graph bootstrap has generator, independent verification, refinement, approval, seeding, migration, and multiple immutable history views; most should not participate in ordinary development.\",\n    \"Thirty-seven detailed contracts and 59 edges over-plan a very small prototype while at least one supposedly single-agent contract still requires substantial human design clarification.\",\n    \"AgentRuntime and TaskExecution create nested immutable audit artifacts but cannot yet edit or validate the game.\",\n    \"Planner, evaluator, validator, fresh diff reviewer, GER evaluator, and human reviewer roles overlap; one worker, deterministic tests, one fresh review, and one human experiential gate are sufficient initially.\",\n    \"A 9,216-word GDD has a dedicated roughly 1,200-line production RAG implementation. The existing retriever is usable, but expanding it into a context/scanner platform is not yet justified.\",\n    \"Provider migration and eight-role ArchitectureReview execution consumed attention without increasing gameplay throughput.\",\n    \"Multiple handoff documents repeat mutable current-state narratives and have already diverged.\"\n  ],\n  \"state_and_authority_concerns\": [\n    \"Necessary boundaries are: human-approved design/decisions; bounded task intent; integrated main as implementation truth; test/runtime evidence; and explicit human experiential/merge approval.\",\n    \"The GDD should not own volatile repository facts. Canonical scene paths, installed components, and build state belong to Git/project inspection or a generated inventory.\",\n    \"Task contracts should reference relevant canon rather than duplicate long excerpts and bootstrap repository observations as quasi-current state.\",\n    \"Reconciliation snapshots are useful historical audits but duplicate the living task graph when treated as a routine synchronization mechanism.\",\n    \"The task contract, conformance record, test XML, TaskExecution request, AgentRuntime request/result, provider log, and future supervisor journal risk becoming overlapping audit stores. One attempt manifest plus raw evidence is enough.\",\n    \"Conformance checks hash consistency, not semantic correctness: NSC-023 can be reported conformant while current canon still identifies the deleted scene as authoritative.\",\n    \"The evaluator checks current HEAD rather than an explicit integration ref, so it cannot independently prove 'merged to main.'\",\n    \"The schema-v2 correction removed unsafe mutable completion but left readiness entirely disabled; the work graph is durable planning data, not an operational autonomy engine.\",\n    \"START_HERE, CURRENT_STATE, master context, milestone contexts, and ADRs duplicate current state. Durable documentation only helps if one compact status file is canonical and mechanically checked.\"\n  ],\n  \"strong_decisions\": [\n    \"Deterministic tools establish facts; model claims do not.\",\n    \"Keep implementation workers bounded to one approved task.\",\n    \"Start with one worker and isolated branches/worktrees.\",\n    \"Treat Unity/runtime evidence as first-class and retain human game-feel/visual checks.\",\n    \"Use bounded repair loops with no-progress detection and circuit breakers.\",\n    \"Do not let implementation agents silently invent missing design.\",\n    \"Protect canonical assets from test mutation and distinguish builder capability from committed-artifact state.\",\n    \"Keep reconciliation snapshots historical and require review before graph changes.\",\n    \"Derive completion/conformance from committed evidence rather than editable task status.\",\n    \"Defer parallelism and automatic merge until repeated single-ticket delivery succeeds.\"\n  ],\n  \"structural_flaws\": [\n    \"There is no vertical path from task selection through writable execution, Unity validation, recovery, merge, and readiness. The architecture currently stops before development begins.\",\n    \"Moving-target synchronization is not implemented. A scene relocation immediately left canon, task contracts, project requirements, and resource locks stale.\",\n    \"The structural validator accepts nonexistent operational resource paths and semantically obsolete task contracts.\",\n    \"The GDD is both design canon and a repository-state narrative, creating an irresolvable authority collision when implementation changes first.\",\n    \"The entire GDD hash is simultaneously over-conservative for unrelated edits and insufficient to prove semantic applicability.\",\n    \"The current conformance evaluator does not establish integration into main despite documentation using integrated main as authority.\",\n    \"NSC-003 is labeled concrete and single-agent while the repository's own current-state document lists unresolved design and integration questions.\",\n    \"Status documentation is not reliably resumable because the designated entry documents contradict each other at the same commit.\",\n    \"Infrastructure milestone ordering has optimized local correctness of individual subsystems rather than end-to-end player-value throughput.\"\n  ],\n  \"throughput_assessment\": \"Current leverage is low. The system can validate graph structure, retrieve current GDD chunks, invoke read-only providers, and derive one retrospective camera baseline, but it cannot autonomously select, edit, test, merge, or complete gameplay work. Repository history shows large infrastructure throughput but essentially no new gameplay after Assignment 6. If forbidden to build infrastructure for one slice, the first break is immediate: AgentRuntime cannot write or execute approved commands and taskcontrol cannot authorize a task. Bypassing those gates manually, NSC-003 then breaks on unresolved input/pathing semantics and the need for host Unity validation. Assignment 6 suggests bounded agents plus Unity feedback can help, but material speed should not be claimed until at least two consecutive gameplay slices use the same thin delivery lane without new orchestration code.\",\n  \"verdict_reason\": \"The project has selected several correct safety principles, but the implemented architecture is not fundamentally sound as an operational autonomous-development system yet. Its authorities already drift, semantic synchronization is missing, conformance does not prove integration, and the graph cannot dispatch. At the same time, substantial speculative machinery has displaced gameplay. Reorganize around a minimal, moving-canon-aware, one-ticket Unity delivery loop and make every further subsystem earn itself through an observed failure.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"better_next_step_if_any\":\"A key fault line has emerged: the synthesis adopts the NSC-005 minority and proposes new requirement IDs, scoped impact sets, a restartable journal, typed receipts, and a preflight as if these are a minimal lane. I’m now checking whether that “minimal” slice is actually playable and whether those new authorities reduce risk or merely replace conservative checks with unauditable human mappings.\",\"failure_if_followed\":[],\"final_assessment\":\"synthesis_holds\",\"hidden_assumptions\":[\"The existing mana feature exposes a real player-accessible denied-spend path suitable for human Play Mode proof.\",\"Human-reviewed requirement impact sets will be complete enough to safely replace whole-GDD invalidation.\",\"A new journal/receipt/preflight can be introduced without becoming another competing control plane.\"],\"ignored_minority_arguments\":[\"Several reviewers favored NSC-003 because it exercises foundational Input Actions, scene integration, and dependency propagation; the synthesis chose the easier lane largely to isolate pipeline mechanics.\"],\"reason\":\"These are provisional attack hypotheses; primary Unity and task evidence will determine whether they hold.\",\"strongest_counter_case\":\"\",\"synthesis_weaknesses\":[],\"what_would_change_my_mind\":[]}"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n --glob '\"'!**/*.meta'\"' -i 'player.?mana|spend|insufficient|denied|reset|debug' Assets/NoSafeCircle Tasks/NSC-005.yaml Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\nrg -n 'PlayerMana|PlayerManaUI|Mana' Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n --glob '\"'!**/*.meta'\"' -i 'player.?mana|spend|insufficient|denied|reset|debug' Assets/NoSafeCircle Tasks/NSC-005.yaml Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\nrg -n 'PlayerMana|PlayerManaUI|Mana' Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\"","aggregated_output":"Tasks/NSC-005.yaml:6:  \"title\": \"Player Mana Ownership, Restart Reset, and Denied-Cast Feedback\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:7:  \"reconciliation_key\": \"player-mana\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:11:  \"execution_reason\": \"Bounded addition to one existing component (PlayerMana.cs) that does not require new scene-wired UI, since the existing PlayerManaUI/fill Image can consume a new denied-cast event without builder changes.\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:13:  \"decomposition_reason\": \"Required reset and denied-cast-signal behavior are fully specified by current GDD text without inventing new design; exact feedback presentation is explicitly an implementation choice.\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:20:      \"requirement\": \"Exposes an owner-controlled reset entry point that restores current mana to full and clears post-cast regeneration-delay timer state for floor restart.\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:25:      \"requirement\": \"Exposes a signal (e.g. an event) when a cast attempt is denied due to insufficient mana, distinct from a successful spend, so a consuming spell/UI can present readable low-mana feedback; exact presentation is an implementation choice.\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:29:      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Player Mana ownership\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:37:      \"requirement\": \"Existing Play Mode coverage of Spend/regen/post-cast-delay behavior (PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs) continues to pass and is extended to cover the new reset entry point and denied-cast signal.\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:54:      \"reference\": \"GDD §2 Resources, Feedback, and Failure — Player Mana ownership\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:55:      \"requirement\": \"The shared Player Mana system is the single owner of current mana and the post-cast regeneration-delay state; spells spend mana through its owner-controlled spend interface; Player Mana does not own spell-local cooldown/charge/cast/placement/active-field state.\"\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:59:      \"requirement\": \"Player Mana owns current mana and post-cast regeneration-delay state and exposes an owner-controlled spend/reset interface to spells and restart orchestration.\"\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:63:      \"requirement\": \"Player Mana and its regeneration-delay state are required reset participants for the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator.\"\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:69:  \"notes\": \"Force Wave's 25-mana cost and other spell-local spend amounts remain owned by the individual spells, not this item; this item only owns the shared pool, regen-delay, spend interface, reset, and denied-cast signal.\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:73:      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerMana.cs\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:75:      \"observation\": \"Implements CurrentMana/MaxMana, Spend(amount) with silent-fail-on-insufficient behavior, Tick-driven post-cast-delay regeneration, and a ManaSpent event; no reset entry point and no denied-cast event exist.\"\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:78:      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/PlayerManaUI.cs\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:85:      \"observation\": \"Both PlayerMana.cs (guid fd54c6f00e974856b70833ac4771b9ae) and PlayerManaUI.cs (guid 0a03ab321b0847f59d3f91214dabcf5c) are serialized and wired on the Player/Canvas GameObjects, confirming the continuous mana indicator is currently integrated, not just buildable.\"\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:88:      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs\",\r\nTasks/NSC-005.yaml:90:      \"observation\": \"Real Play Mode tests cover Spend success/failure, post-cast-delay non-regeneration, regeneration after delay, and delay-reset on subsequent spend.\"\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:55:2. Avoid the group, pull part of it away, or spend mana on a direct fight.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:68:| Open Sealed Door | Click the intended sealed door to request movement to its interaction position. The wizard walks there automatically. When the wizard reaches arm's-reach interaction range, the five-second opening timer begins automatically; no sustained button hold is required. Cursor drift does not matter after selection. Taking damage, moving away, or issuing another command that cancels/replaces the door approach resets the attempt. If uninterrupted for five seconds, the door opens. | Makes each exit a positioning challenge while allowing one click to mean \"go to this door and start opening it when I arrive.\" |\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:79:The three spells solve different problems: Frost Field controls pursuit, Force Wave repairs an immediate positioning mistake, and Charged Fireball converts a previously created opening into damage. No single spell reliably answers both enemy types by itself. Force Wave's cooldown is tuned so an encounter space typically allows only about one meaningful use: spending it to escape a mid-room surround may leave none available for that room's door attempt, and saving it for the door means resolving mid-room danger without it. This is an intentional resource decision, not proof that either use was wrong.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:83:- Health: The wizard survives a few isolated hits but dies quickly when trapped. Health does not regenerate during a room, and does not reset between rooms — it carries across the whole run. Successfully locking a door after crossing restores a small, fixed amount of health before the next room begins; this recovery is partial, so accumulated damage still matters across all five rooms. Exact recovery values will be set during playtesting.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:87:- Player Mana ownership: The shared Player Mana system is the single owner of current mana and the post-cast regeneration-delay state. Spells spend mana through its owner-controlled spend interface. Player Mana does not own spell-local cooldown, charge, cast, placement, or active-effect state merely because those spells consume mana.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:91:- Force Wave: Each cast spends **25 mana** through Player Mana and also starts its visible long cooldown. Twenty-five mana is the initial implementation/balance value; later playtesting may change the number, but Force Wave remains a mana-consuming spell unless the GDD is revised.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:92:- Spell-local state ownership: Fireball owns its tap/charge/cast state, Frost Field owns its Wizard-Combat-side cast/placement/active-field state, and Force Wave owns its cooldown state. Enemy-side Frost slowdown application/restoration remains owned by Enemy Pursuit/status-effect logic. Each owner exposes a reset entry point for any owned state that can still be active when a floor restart occurs.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:94:- Setbacks: Once the wizard has reached the selected door and the five-second timer has begun, taking damage, moving away, or issuing another command that cancels/replaces the door interaction resets progress. Releasing the mouse button does not matter because the door interaction does not require a sustained hold. Cursor movement away from the selected door after the initial click does not reset progress.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:100:**Victory/input-shutdown ownership:** A reusable Game Flow/Victory capability owns the transition into the won state, display of the **You Escaped** overlay, and coordination of gameplay shutdown. Player Movement, Door/Interaction, Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave each expose an owner-controlled gameplay-enable/suspend interface that can immediately stop or cancel their current input-driven activity, reject new gameplay commands while suspended, and be re-enabled by an authorized reset/test flow. The victory capability consumes those interfaces rather than mutating another system's internal state. Disabling an Input Actions map may be part of the implementation, but the runtime contract must also stop already-active movement, door-opening, charging/casting, or other input-driven activity rather than merely preventing the next button press.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:102:**Loss:** Reach zero health. The floor then restarts from the beginning. This is an intentional, single-attempt structure — reaching zero health at any point restarts the entire floor by design, not as a placeholder. Restart resets all run-persistent gameplay state to the floor's initial state, including Player Health, Player Mana and its regeneration-delay state, player position/movement state, Fireball charge/cast state, Frost Field casting/active-field state, Force Wave cooldown state, persistent enemy objects and enemy health, pursuit/search/status/attack state, Active Enemy Registry bookkeeping, door lifecycle/crossing/durability state, and encounter activation/admission state. Each persistent enemy is returned to the authored encounter/spawn region associated with its original room and starts again in its initial AI state; the exact coordinate within that authored region may vary, but no target, last-known-position, search, attack, status-effect, or displacement state carries over from the failed run.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:104:**Floor-run restart ownership:** Player Health's observable zero-health/death transition triggers the shared Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator, which owns starting a fresh floor attempt and coordinating the reset. It does not reach into or duplicate another system's internal state. Every system that owns state that can persist or remain active when restart occurs must expose a reset entry point for the state it owns, and the orchestrator invokes those owned reset interfaces. Required reset participants include Player Health, Player Mana, Player Movement/player position, Fireball, Frost Field's casting-side state, Force Wave, enemy health/defeat state, enemy pursuit/search/attack/status/displacement state, Active Enemy Registry bookkeeping, door lifecycle/crossing/durability state, and encounter activation/admission state. Enemy reset returns each persistent enemy to its original authored encounter/spawn region and initial AI state, with no retained target knowledge from the failed run; exact placement within that authored region is an encounter implementation detail. Early implementation may validate the orchestrator against only the persistent systems that currently exist, but full restart closure remains required work until every implemented state owner participates; adding later rooms or stateful systems must extend the reset participants without redesigning the orchestration contract.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:131:**Enemy health and defeat ownership:** A reusable **Enemy Health/Defeat** capability owns each persistent enemy object's current health, damage intake, defeat transition, and restartable health/defeat state. Fireball and any other canon-required damage source request damage through this owner-controlled interface rather than writing enemy health directly. When defeat occurs, the Enemy Health/Defeat owner reports that transition through the **Active Enemy Registry** owner's removal/unregister interface so the active count is updated without duplicating registry bookkeeping. Floor restart restores enemy health/defeat state through the enemy-owned reset entry point; the Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator does not mutate enemy-health internals directly.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:140:- If the bounded search completes without reacquisition, the enemy clears the target and returns to its local idle/wander behavior. Losing the target does not despawn, replace, or reset the enemy; it remains the same persistent enemy object and can be encountered again later.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:147:- A sealed door is a cursor-targeted interactable. Clicking the intended door issues a combined approach-and-interact request: the wizard moves to that door's interaction position, and the five-second opening timer begins automatically when the wizard reaches arm's-reach range. No sustained button hold is required. After the door is selected, cursor drift does not cancel the request or the timer. Taking damage, moving away after timing begins, or issuing another command that cancels/replaces the door interaction resets the attempt.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:201:| Door and Interaction Agent | Implements cursor-targeted door selection, click-to-approach movement request consumption, arm's-reach arrival validation, automatic five-second opening timing, interruption, shared doorway-crossing state, automatic closing/locking after forward-side crossing, runtime door durability, the owner-controlled door-damage receive interface, and breaking. It is the single owner of detecting when the wizard crosses to the forward side of an opened door; locking and final escape consume that state. Enemy attack behavior requests door damage through the Door-owned interface instead of writing durability or lifecycle state directly. After a door is clicked, cursor movement off the selected door does not cancel the approach or timer; after timing begins, damage, moving away, or issuing another command that cancels/replaces the interaction resets it. No sustained interaction hold is required. | Makes room exits risky and safety temporary without requiring sustained pointer precision after selection, while preventing multiple systems from inventing separate doorway-crossing or door-durability ownership. |\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:202:| Enemy Pursuit Agent | Implements detection, melee and ranged attacks, pursuit, movement through open doors, distance-based target loss, last-known-position search, bounded randomized search/wander, and reacquisition, including applying and restoring Frost Field's slowdown effect. Detection Distance is smaller than Lose Target Distance; crossing a doorway does not itself clear pursuit. Enemy attacks consume the shared Player Health damage interface rather than owning separate player-health state. Owns the reusable Enemy Health/Defeat capability for persistent enemy objects: damage sources consume its damage interface, defeat reports removal through the Active Enemy Registry interface, and floor restart consumes its reset interface. Owns Ranged Enemy targeting, attack timing, and line-of-sight/projectile-occlusion checks, so Chapel of Ash's cover actually blocks shots. Enemy locomotion consumes the shared gameplay navigation/locomotion layer rather than choosing or configuring that layer independently. Owns the shared Active Enemy Registry bookkeeping for persistent active enemy objects; encounter activation consumes that registry rather than maintaining a separate count. Owns the ongoing state of persistent enemy objects whether they are actively pursuing, searching, or later reacquired. It also owns the application of forced enemy displacement requested by abilities such as Force Wave, including returning affected enemies to the appropriate pursuit/search movement state afterward. Qualifying locked-door attacks are initiated by this enemy behavior but apply durability loss only through the Door and Interaction damage interface. | Makes enemies left alive remain a visible, persistent consequence while preserving explicit ownership of enemy health/defeat, player-health damage, navigation, registry bookkeeping, and Door-owned durability. |\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:211:- **Wizard Combat Agent** owns player movement/resources, shared pointer projection, and spell initiation. Within that responsibility, Player Health, Player Mana, Player Movement, and each spell remain separate runtime state owners rather than collapsing their state into one shared player system. Player Health owns health and its zero-health transition; Player Mana owns mana and regeneration-delay state; Player Movement owns position/locomotion, movement-restriction state, and shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection; Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave own their spell-local state. Charged Fireball consumes the Player Movement restriction interface instead of mutating movement internals. Spells consume Player Mana through its spend interface; Force Wave's initial cost is 25 mana per cast. Wizard Combat requests enemy effects through enemy-owned interfaces and does not directly manipulate enemy locomotion, status restoration, or forced-displacement state.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:214:- **Final escape/victory coordination** owns only the won-state transition, gameplay-suspension coordination, and victory presentation. Player Movement, Door/Interaction, Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave remain owners of their own active behavior and must expose owner-controlled suspend/re-enable entry points consumed by victory/reset flows. Final escape logic must not reach into movement, door, or spell internals to stop them.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:247:No Safe Circle will be developed by one developer in Unity using C#. A raw Python orchestration pipeline using Claude Code agents will coordinate specialized development agents that assist with planning, implementation, review, debugging, and test design. Independent tasks may run at the same time, but tasks that modify the same scripts, scenes, or prefabs will be completed sequentially.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:296:- A reusable **Enemy Health/Defeat** runtime capability owns each persistent enemy's current health, damage intake, defeat transition, and floor-restart reset. Fireball and any other canon-required enemy-damage source call that capability's damage interface. Defeat then reports removal through the Active Enemy Registry's owner-controlled interface; neither damage sources nor the restart orchestrator directly write enemy-health or registry internals.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:300:- A reusable **Game Flow/Victory** capability consumes the final door's shared forward-side crossing state, owns the won-state transition and **You Escaped** presentation, and coordinates gameplay suspension through owner-controlled interfaces. Player Movement, Door/Interaction, Fireball, Frost Field, and Force Wave must each provide a suspend/re-enable entry point that stops current input-driven activity as well as future commands. The victory capability does not directly mutate locomotion, door-interaction, spell-charge, cast, cooldown, or other owner-internal state.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:301:- A shared **Floor Run/Restart Orchestrator** coordinates a new floor attempt when it receives Player Health's zero-health/death transition. It invokes reset entry points owned by each stateful system rather than directly mutating their internals. Player Health, Player Mana, Player Movement/player position, spell-local Fireball/Frost Field/Force Wave state, enemy health and pursuit/search/attack/status/displacement state, Active Enemy Registry bookkeeping, door lifecycle/crossing/durability state, and encounter activation/admission state must all participate once those systems exist. Enemy reset returns each persistent enemy to the authored encounter/spawn region associated with its original room and clears all knowledge/state from the failed run; exact placement inside that region may vary. Early staged restart implementation is valid only as an incremental step; the persistent-systems closure remains required until all implemented state owners are connected to the orchestrator.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:302:- Player Mana owns current mana and the post-cast regeneration-delay state and exposes an owner-controlled spend/reset interface to spells and restart orchestration. Force Wave spends 25 mana per cast through this interface as its initial tuning value. Spell-local cooldown/charge/cast/active-field state stays on the spell that owns it. Player Movement owns position/locomotion, the shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection, and the owner-controlled movement-restriction interface consumed by Charged Fireball. Runtime actions are supplied through Unity Input System/Input Actions rather than independent direct hardware polling inside gameplay systems.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:308:The project will use a maximum planning budget of 1.5 million combined input and output tokens across approximately thirty to forty development tasks. This is a spending ceiling rather than a required amount.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:310:Most focused tasks will be limited to approximately 15,000–30,000 tokens. Larger context will be reserved for integration, debugging, and reviewing systems that interact with several Unity components.\r\nDocs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:318:| Review, debugging, and validation | 400,000 |\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:8:    public class PlayerManaPlayModeTests\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:11:        private PlayerMana mana;\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:17:            mana = manaObject.AddComponent<PlayerMana>();\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:27:        public IEnumerator Spend_ReducesCurrentMana_WhenSufficient()\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:31:            var success = mana.Spend(20f);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:40:        public IEnumerator Spend_Fails_WhenAmountExceedsCurrentMana()\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:44:            var success = mana.Spend(before + 1f);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:55:            mana.Spend(30f);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:56:            var afterSpend = mana.CurrentMana;\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:62:            Assert.AreEqual(afterSpend, mana.CurrentMana, 0.001f);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:68:            mana.Spend(30f);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:69:            var afterSpend = mana.CurrentMana;\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:75:            Assert.Greater(mana.CurrentMana, afterSpend);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:79:        public IEnumerator Spend_DuringRegen_ResetsPostCastDelay()\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:81:            mana.Spend(10f);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:85:            mana.Spend(10f);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:86:            var afterSecondSpend = mana.CurrentMana;\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:91:            Assert.Less(afterSecondSpend, regeneratedMana);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:92:            Assert.AreEqual(afterSecondSpend, mana.CurrentMana, 0.001f);\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/PlayerManaPlayModeTests.cs:95:        private static void AdvanceManaTime(PlayerMana target, float totalSeconds)\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/DebugManaSpendControl.cs:7:    /// mana spend to demonstrate the pool's spend/post-cast-delay/regen behavior standalone,\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/DebugManaSpendControl.cs:9:    public class DebugManaSpendControl : MonoBehaviour\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/DebugManaSpendControl.cs:11:        [SerializeField] private PlayerMana target;\r\nAssets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/